74
00:04:49,590 --> 00:04:58,710
So last time, I think that you were learning about some aspects

75
00:04:58,710 --> 00:05:01,550
of representation learning.

76
00:05:01,550 --> 00:05:06,370
And there was this idea of developing techniques,

77
00:05:06,370 --> 00:05:09,870
contrastive learning techniques where

78
00:05:09,870 --> 00:05:12,270
you try to learn embeddings of your data.

79
00:05:12,270 --> 00:05:18,830
And you specifically want to take basically similar data

80
00:05:18,830 --> 00:05:23,527
points and map them to nearby embeddings.

81
00:05:23,527 --> 00:05:25,110
And then you want to take a data point

82
00:05:25,110 --> 00:05:27,990
from a very different class and map it

83
00:05:27,990 --> 00:05:30,010
to an embedding that's far away.

84
00:05:32,790 --> 00:05:36,110
And we call these contrastive learning algorithms.

85
00:05:36,110 --> 00:05:39,190
And they're good ways to learn representations.

86
00:05:46,738 --> 00:05:49,030
Sometimes we think of them as somehow unsupervised ways

87
00:05:49,030 --> 00:05:50,210
to learn representations.

88
00:05:50,210 --> 00:05:51,810
But anyway, that was last lecture.

89
00:05:55,020 --> 00:05:59,260
And then I also wanted to remind you

90
00:05:59,260 --> 00:06:01,840
that in even the earlier lectures,

91
00:06:01,840 --> 00:06:03,883
we were thinking about different perspectives

92
00:06:03,883 --> 00:06:05,300
on thinking about what's happening

93
00:06:05,300 --> 00:06:08,220
inside a neural network.

94
00:06:08,220 --> 00:06:12,060
And there was the idea of neural perspective,

95
00:06:12,060 --> 00:06:13,900
thinking about tensors, and then thinking

96
00:06:13,900 --> 00:06:17,880
about spectral properties of tensors.

97
00:06:21,260 --> 00:06:25,900
So this is just recapping some different things.

98
00:06:25,900 --> 00:06:32,220
But in this lecture, not really using that,

99
00:06:32,220 --> 00:06:36,240
and just thinking more abstractly of a neural network

100
00:06:36,240 --> 00:06:42,000
as being a map from a sequence of vector spaces.

101
00:06:42,000 --> 00:06:43,580
So every layer of the neural network,

102
00:06:43,580 --> 00:06:45,600
you can think of it as a vector space.

103
00:06:48,500 --> 00:06:53,540
And one possible goal of doing deep learning

104
00:06:53,540 --> 00:06:57,260
is to learn a really good representation of your data

105
00:06:57,260 --> 00:06:58,260
points.

106
00:06:58,260 --> 00:07:05,700
And I'm just visualizing that here as the idea of the input

107
00:07:05,700 --> 00:07:09,420
space, maybe all the different data points of unrelated things

108
00:07:09,420 --> 00:07:12,620
are all mixed up together and not really

109
00:07:12,620 --> 00:07:14,860
separated or disentangled.

110
00:07:14,860 --> 00:07:16,880
And then when we do deep learning,

111
00:07:16,880 --> 00:07:20,340
we're hoping that as we move through the network,

112
00:07:20,340 --> 00:07:23,380
we get to the representation at the end

113
00:07:23,380 --> 00:07:25,740
where the data points from different classes

114
00:07:25,740 --> 00:07:28,020
or semantically different data points are

115
00:07:28,020 --> 00:07:29,660
separated from each other.

116
00:07:29,660 --> 00:07:32,920
And then we did a good job.

117
00:07:35,717 --> 00:07:37,300
So that's what this is saying, that we

118
00:07:37,300 --> 00:07:39,743
want a representation of our data

119
00:07:39,743 --> 00:07:41,660
which is perhaps linearly separable at the end

120
00:07:41,660 --> 00:07:42,400
of the network.

121
00:07:42,400 --> 00:07:43,360
That would be nice.

122
00:07:47,980 --> 00:07:49,980
So what I want to talk about in this lecture

123
00:07:49,980 --> 00:07:55,220
is the idea that when you pick what your neural network

124
00:07:55,220 --> 00:08:01,180
architecture is, it already has an opinion about which data

125
00:08:01,180 --> 00:08:04,680
points are similar to each other and which ones are dissimilar,

126
00:08:04,680 --> 00:08:08,780
even without doing contrastive learning.

127
00:08:08,780 --> 00:08:12,960
So I want to just introduce some tools for thinking about this.

128
00:08:17,460 --> 00:08:23,060
And that's all this is saying, is that the neural architecture

129
00:08:23,060 --> 00:08:24,980
already expresses some kind of opinion

130
00:08:24,980 --> 00:08:27,660
about how similar two data points are,

131
00:08:27,660 --> 00:08:30,340
even before you train it.

132
00:08:30,340 --> 00:08:34,080
And we'll think about some tools to capture that idea.

133
00:08:38,659 --> 00:08:41,820
By the way, as ever, please just interrupt me

134
00:08:41,820 --> 00:08:43,120
if you have questions.

135
00:08:51,540 --> 00:09:01,540
So the idea here is, I just give you some data.

136
00:09:01,540 --> 00:09:04,100
And I just want you to think about,

137
00:09:04,100 --> 00:09:07,460
just from first principles, ignoring everything you've ever

138
00:09:07,460 --> 00:09:10,740
learned in machine learning, about how you would actually

139
00:09:10,740 --> 00:09:14,780
fit it if you wanted to fit a model to this data.

140
00:09:14,780 --> 00:09:16,440
Does anyone have ideas?

141
00:09:20,300 --> 00:09:24,220
