144
00:09:30,220 --> 00:09:33,020
JEREMY BERNSTEIN: That would kind of do that.

145
00:09:33,020 --> 00:09:37,000
Yeah, that's one idea, just linear interpolation.

146
00:09:47,890 --> 00:09:49,510
Yeah, I feel like--

147
00:09:49,510 --> 00:09:50,270
Yeah, go ahead.

148
00:09:50,270 --> 00:09:52,810
AUDIENCE: [INAUDIBLE] interpolation.

149
00:09:52,810 --> 00:09:54,770
JEREMY BERNSTEIN: Polynomial interpolation,

150
00:09:54,770 --> 00:09:57,170
which is maybe harder to draw, but maybe I could fit

151
00:09:57,170 --> 00:09:59,270
some kind of quadratic to this.

152
00:10:02,210 --> 00:10:03,050
Potentially.

153
00:10:07,435 --> 00:10:08,810
If you just think about this, you

154
00:10:08,810 --> 00:10:11,210
can invent a lot of different function classes

155
00:10:11,210 --> 00:10:13,010
and just think about what it would

156
00:10:13,010 --> 00:10:17,270
mean to do machine learning with different function classes.

157
00:10:17,270 --> 00:10:21,997
And it's kind of a big jump to get to doing neural networks,

158
00:10:21,997 --> 00:10:23,830
if you just think about things from scratch.

159
00:10:23,830 --> 00:10:29,290
But that's kind of where we are.

160
00:10:29,290 --> 00:10:34,570
So basically, there are a lot of classic approaches to doing

161
00:10:34,570 --> 00:10:36,730
machine learning, where people did just

162
00:10:36,730 --> 00:10:38,950
invent their favorite function class and say, hey,

163
00:10:38,950 --> 00:10:41,367
we're just going to do machine learning with this function

164
00:10:41,367 --> 00:10:42,970
class.

165
00:10:42,970 --> 00:10:48,870
So a really classic example is called kernel methods.

166
00:10:48,870 --> 00:10:50,850
And actually it's quite related to what

167
00:10:50,850 --> 00:10:53,790
we talked about in the approximation theory lecture,

168
00:10:53,790 --> 00:10:57,090
which was maybe lecture two.

169
00:10:57,090 --> 00:10:58,930
But kernel method says we're going

170
00:10:58,930 --> 00:11:02,370
to take a kernel, which is basically,

171
00:11:02,370 --> 00:11:04,510
you could also think of it as a bump function.

172
00:11:08,130 --> 00:11:11,970
And it could just look like a little Gaussian

173
00:11:11,970 --> 00:11:14,210
or a little curvy.

174
00:11:14,210 --> 00:11:19,730
And I'm going to place one kernel

175
00:11:19,730 --> 00:11:28,010
on the location of each input, if this makes sense.

176
00:11:28,010 --> 00:11:32,830
So if I've got four inputs, I'll put four little bump functions.

177
00:11:32,830 --> 00:11:35,787
And intuitively, if I've got four data points

178
00:11:35,787 --> 00:11:37,370
and I have four bump functions, then I

179
00:11:37,370 --> 00:11:40,390
have enough degrees of freedom to fit the data.

180
00:11:40,390 --> 00:11:43,670
So that seems to make sense.

181
00:11:43,670 --> 00:11:47,930
And then I'm just going to rescale them

182
00:11:47,930 --> 00:11:56,190
with scalar coefficients just so that they interpolate the data.

183
00:11:56,190 --> 00:11:59,330
So it's four bump functions, four scalar coefficients,

184
00:11:59,330 --> 00:12:00,430
four data points.

185
00:12:00,430 --> 00:12:01,630
Everything checks out.

186
00:12:06,010 --> 00:12:10,890
And I wrote that down here.

187
00:12:10,890 --> 00:12:16,330
So I construct my function, f of x, if I've got n data

188
00:12:16,330 --> 00:12:19,730
points, I need to sum over n bump functions.

189
00:12:19,730 --> 00:12:24,950
And I denote each bump function using this notation k.

190
00:12:28,150 --> 00:12:30,710
And then there's also a scalar alpha,

191
00:12:30,710 --> 00:12:33,670
which allows me to reweight the heights of the different bumps.

192
00:12:33,670 --> 00:12:37,370
And then if I have n bumps and n data points,

193
00:12:37,370 --> 00:12:39,210
I can definitely fit my data.

194
00:12:39,210 --> 00:12:41,530
So it's kind of a natural way just

195
00:12:41,530 --> 00:12:49,330
to explain a little bit about what k of x and xi means.

196
00:12:49,330 --> 00:13:01,370
Roughly, looking at this, this would be k of x, 0.

197
00:13:01,370 --> 00:13:04,530
And then you should think about this xi

198
00:13:04,530 --> 00:13:08,250
as allowing me to translate this bump along the x-axis

199
00:13:08,250 --> 00:13:11,790
to be centered on that i-th data point.

200
00:13:20,650 --> 00:13:22,690
That's just what this bit says.

201
00:13:22,690 --> 00:13:27,330
And then I can define a function space, let's call it big F,

202
00:13:27,330 --> 00:13:30,410
to be the set of functions f that

203
00:13:30,410 --> 00:13:33,390
can be decomposed in that form.

204
00:13:40,850 --> 00:13:45,560
And if I let the number of bump functions

205
00:13:45,560 --> 00:13:51,400
actually go to infinity, so I consider all possible locations

206
00:13:51,400 --> 00:13:53,160
of where I put the bumps, and then

207
00:13:53,160 --> 00:13:57,120
I actually allow myself to have infinite bumps.

208
00:13:57,120 --> 00:14:01,720
Then, this is what is known as, they

209
00:14:01,720 --> 00:14:04,360
call this function space a reproducing kernel Hilbert

210
00:14:04,360 --> 00:14:05,020
space.

211
00:14:09,480 --> 00:14:12,640
And this was all the rage before deep learning

212
00:14:12,640 --> 00:14:17,720
or before the recent, let's say the last 13 years

213
00:14:17,720 --> 00:14:19,440
of deep learning.

214
00:14:19,440 --> 00:14:21,860
Before that, everyone was doing kernel methods,

215
00:14:21,860 --> 00:14:24,800
and they were considering spaces of functions like this.

216
00:14:24,800 --> 00:14:26,900
Instead of choosing your neural architecture,

217
00:14:26,900 --> 00:14:30,320
the whole thing was like, you pick a kernel function.

218
00:14:30,320 --> 00:14:32,240
You choose a good kernel or a good bump

219
00:14:32,240 --> 00:14:37,240
function that somehow captures the structure in your data.

220
00:14:37,240 --> 00:14:40,460
And all the machine learning-- sorry, slight exaggeration,

221
00:14:40,460 --> 00:14:44,760
but there were a lot of machine learning papers focused

222
00:14:44,760 --> 00:14:46,020
on kernel methods.

223
00:14:49,280 --> 00:14:55,480
So that's just a little introduction to kernel methods.

224
00:14:55,480 --> 00:14:59,000
And if you just think about it, it's not so obvious

225
00:14:59,000 --> 00:15:01,000
why do we use neural networks.

226
00:15:01,000 --> 00:15:04,560
Why didn't we go back to kernel methods?

227
00:15:04,560 --> 00:15:07,460
You need to think about it or it may not be obvious.

228
00:15:11,160 --> 00:15:14,000
There's a second way, second classic way

229
00:15:14,000 --> 00:15:23,040
of constructing function spaces or doing machine learning, where

230
00:15:23,040 --> 00:15:29,300
actually this time, you think about sampling random functions.

231
00:15:29,300 --> 00:15:35,940
So you come up with some way of just sampling random functions.

232
00:15:35,940 --> 00:15:38,680
We would call this some kind of stochastic process.

233
00:15:38,680 --> 00:15:43,560
And imagine you just keep sampling random functions

234
00:15:43,560 --> 00:15:45,840
from your stochastic process.

235
00:15:45,840 --> 00:15:50,520
And if it doesn't fit the data, you just throw it away.

236
00:15:50,520 --> 00:15:53,080
But if it fits the data, you actually keep it.

237
00:15:53,080 --> 00:15:55,160
And you're going to somehow end up

238
00:15:55,160 --> 00:16:04,480
with this distribution of functions that actually goes

239
00:16:04,480 --> 00:16:05,940
through your training data.

240
00:16:05,940 --> 00:16:09,020
So if you actually use this method,

241
00:16:09,020 --> 00:16:10,760
you wouldn't actually do that procedure

242
00:16:10,760 --> 00:16:12,460
of sampling lots of functions.

243
00:16:12,460 --> 00:16:14,360
You would actually do something called

244
00:16:14,360 --> 00:16:17,800
conditioning the distribution of functions on the training data

245
00:16:17,800 --> 00:16:21,080
and getting what we would call the posterior

246
00:16:21,080 --> 00:16:24,480
distribution of functions.

247
00:16:24,480 --> 00:16:27,380
But intuitively, this is what's going on with this technique.

248
00:16:30,480 --> 00:16:32,520
And there's a special way to make

249
00:16:32,520 --> 00:16:34,480
this computationally tractable.

250
00:16:34,480 --> 00:16:36,960
And it basically amounts to making the way

251
00:16:36,960 --> 00:16:40,720
that you generate the functions like a Gaussian thing.

252
00:16:40,720 --> 00:16:44,000
And then all the computations that you

253
00:16:44,000 --> 00:16:48,020
need to do this procedure become straightforward.

254
00:16:50,690 --> 00:16:51,560
AUDIENCE: Question.

255
00:16:51,560 --> 00:16:52,518
JEREMY BERNSTEIN: Yeah?

256
00:16:52,518 --> 00:16:53,935
AUDIENCE: If I remember correctly,

257
00:16:53,935 --> 00:16:55,720
I think the optimal thing is roughly

258
00:16:55,720 --> 00:16:57,920
like a kernel method itself.

259
00:16:57,920 --> 00:16:59,480
How similar are they?

260
00:16:59,480 --> 00:17:03,160
JEREMY BERNSTEIN: Yeah, that's a really good question.

