00:17:07,000 --> 00:17:08,839
Actually, it turns out, as it happens,

263
00:17:08,839 --> 00:17:12,352
there's a close connection between this procedure

264
00:17:12,352 --> 00:17:14,560
and the thing on the previous slide with all the bump

265
00:17:14,560 --> 00:17:15,720
functions.

266
00:17:15,720 --> 00:17:17,680
And that's kind of interesting.

267
00:17:29,880 --> 00:17:33,340
We've not discussed yet how to sample these random functions,

268
00:17:33,340 --> 00:17:35,220
but we're going to discuss them.

269
00:17:35,220 --> 00:17:39,150
But let's just say that we've discussed it already.

270
00:17:39,150 --> 00:17:41,550
And the point is that if you sample

271
00:17:41,550 --> 00:17:43,350
all of these random functions that

272
00:17:43,350 --> 00:17:45,990
are consistent with the data and then compute

273
00:17:45,990 --> 00:17:48,270
the mean of that distribution, you

274
00:17:48,270 --> 00:17:51,670
get a single, quite smooth function, which

275
00:17:51,670 --> 00:17:55,390
is the mean of that distribution over consistent functions.

276
00:17:55,390 --> 00:18:04,870
And that turns out to be equivalent to the kernel

277
00:18:04,870 --> 00:18:08,590
interpolator of minimum kernel norm,

278
00:18:08,590 --> 00:18:13,790
for some definition of the RKHS norm.

279
00:18:13,790 --> 00:18:16,490
So yeah, there's a really close connection.

280
00:18:16,490 --> 00:18:20,330
But that should not make sense to everyone,

281
00:18:20,330 --> 00:18:23,130
because we haven't defined any of those things.

282
00:18:28,590 --> 00:18:29,230
Oh, yeah.

283
00:18:29,230 --> 00:18:31,270
OK, here was the slide.

284
00:18:31,270 --> 00:18:37,790
So there's three ways now that we're going

285
00:18:37,790 --> 00:18:42,710
to think about function spaces.

286
00:18:42,710 --> 00:18:45,390
So first of all, this one is the one we just

287
00:18:45,390 --> 00:18:48,628
talked about where you sample lots of random functions,

288
00:18:48,628 --> 00:18:51,170
and then you throw away all the ones that don't fit the data,

289
00:18:51,170 --> 00:18:53,590
and you just keep the ones that fit the data.

290
00:18:53,590 --> 00:18:57,910
And this will be something called a Gaussian process.

291
00:18:57,910 --> 00:19:00,510
Kernel methods are the ones with the bump functions, where

292
00:19:00,510 --> 00:19:04,030
we pick our favorite little bump and arrange

293
00:19:04,030 --> 00:19:06,870
them to fit the data.

294
00:19:06,870 --> 00:19:12,110
And then neural networks are what the class is mainly about.

295
00:19:12,110 --> 00:19:15,030
And the cool thing, the really interesting thing

296
00:19:15,030 --> 00:19:18,470
is that there's all these correspondences.

297
00:19:18,470 --> 00:19:21,470
There's sort of a way to get a kernel method from a Gaussian

298
00:19:21,470 --> 00:19:22,430
process.

299
00:19:22,430 --> 00:19:27,870
There's kind of a way to get a Gaussian process--

300
00:19:27,870 --> 00:19:29,143
they're related to each other.

301
00:19:29,143 --> 00:19:30,810
And the relation somehow goes both ways.

302
00:19:30,810 --> 00:19:32,830
That's all I want to say.

303
00:19:32,830 --> 00:19:35,790
But interestingly, there's a way to get a Gaussian process

304
00:19:35,790 --> 00:19:38,210
from a neural network.

305
00:19:41,330 --> 00:19:44,810
Well, there's actually maybe a couple ways to do it.

306
00:19:44,810 --> 00:19:47,590
But the one that we're going to think about,

307
00:19:47,590 --> 00:19:52,230
there's a correspondence where a neural network

308
00:19:52,230 --> 00:19:54,390
in a particular limit is equivalent to a Gaussian

309
00:19:54,390 --> 00:19:55,550
process.

310
00:19:55,550 --> 00:19:59,750
And it's basically when the network is infinitely wide.

311
00:19:59,750 --> 00:20:01,910
So you take the width of all the hidden layers

312
00:20:01,910 --> 00:20:03,870
and you make them infinitely wide.

313
00:20:03,870 --> 00:20:07,730
And then you randomly sample the weights.

314
00:20:10,670 --> 00:20:13,690
So you could think about that as you repeatedly,

315
00:20:13,690 --> 00:20:15,690
you just keep re-initializing your network.

316
00:20:15,690 --> 00:20:17,430
And it keeps giving you random functions.

317
00:20:17,430 --> 00:20:19,590
If the network is infinitely wide,

318
00:20:19,590 --> 00:20:21,230
it turns out that this distribution

319
00:20:21,230 --> 00:20:25,750
is equivalent to something called a Gaussian process.

320
00:20:25,750 --> 00:20:27,630
So these connections, if you want

321
00:20:27,630 --> 00:20:30,990
to understand more theoretically about deep learning,

322
00:20:30,990 --> 00:20:34,030
it's interesting to explore these connections because you

323
00:20:34,030 --> 00:20:38,310
can relate neural networks to these classical machine learning

324
00:20:38,310 --> 00:20:40,610
methods that people really cared a lot about.

325
00:20:44,270 --> 00:20:46,030
Then, you can ask things like, how useful

326
00:20:46,030 --> 00:20:47,510
are these correspondences?

327
00:20:47,510 --> 00:20:50,070
Do the correspondences actually amount to things

328
00:20:50,070 --> 00:20:51,490
which happen in practice?

329
00:20:51,490 --> 00:20:54,250
And that's something else you've got to think about.

330
00:20:54,250 --> 00:20:56,550
But I'm just going to tell you a little bit about one

331
00:20:56,550 --> 00:20:59,730
of the correspondences.

332
00:20:59,730 --> 00:21:00,230
Yeah?

333
00:21:00,230 --> 00:21:02,610
AUDIENCE: These are all equally expressive, right?

334
00:21:02,610 --> 00:21:04,350
Or is there some sense in which one is

335
00:21:04,350 --> 00:21:07,662
more expressive than the other?

336
00:21:07,662 --> 00:21:09,370
JEREMY BERNSTEIN: That's a good question.

337
00:21:09,370 --> 00:21:11,372
So the question was, is there a sense

338
00:21:11,372 --> 00:21:13,330
in which one is more expressive than the other?

339
00:21:13,330 --> 00:21:16,550
Or all the different function spaces equally expressive?

340
00:21:16,550 --> 00:21:19,950
I think they may just be different in some sense.

341
00:21:19,950 --> 00:21:22,630
You can look up and you'll find some literature

342
00:21:22,630 --> 00:21:24,710
that discusses this.

343
00:21:24,710 --> 00:21:27,880
And it's things like the smoothness properties

344
00:21:27,880 --> 00:21:29,630
of the functions in the different function

345
00:21:29,630 --> 00:21:31,470
spaces may just be different.

346
00:21:31,470 --> 00:21:33,100
Like the kernel thing, because it's

347
00:21:33,100 --> 00:21:35,140
built out of bump functions, even though there's

348
00:21:35,140 --> 00:21:36,890
an infinite number of them, they may still

349
00:21:36,890 --> 00:21:39,200
be reasonably smooth functions.

350
00:21:39,200 --> 00:21:40,980
But I think for a Gaussian process,

351
00:21:40,980 --> 00:21:42,680
depending on the covariate structure,

352
00:21:42,680 --> 00:21:44,980
you can have really horrendous functions.

353
00:21:44,980 --> 00:21:47,200
But interestingly, with that said,

354
00:21:47,200 --> 00:21:49,980
if you take the mean of the posterior of the Gaussian

355
00:21:49,980 --> 00:21:52,920
process, it's actually a nice, smooth function.

356
00:21:52,920 --> 00:21:54,640
So it's just different.

357
00:21:54,640 --> 00:21:57,460
I don't know which one is more expressive.

358
00:21:57,460 --> 00:21:59,400
But it's an interesting thing to think about.

359
00:22:06,700 --> 00:22:08,820
OK, let's see.

360
00:22:08,820 --> 00:22:12,740
OK, so now I'm going to introduce slightly more formally

361
00:22:12,740 --> 00:22:14,680
what a Gaussian process actually is.

362
00:22:21,180 --> 00:22:24,200
So this is just a recap of what we just talked about.

363
00:22:24,200 --> 00:22:34,020
But basically, if we have some data,

364
00:22:34,020 --> 00:22:38,780
we can think about sampling lots of, lots of random functions

365
00:22:38,780 --> 00:22:42,060
that are the ones that are consistent with the data.

366
00:22:42,060 --> 00:22:45,140
And we can get this distribution.

367
00:22:45,140 --> 00:22:46,740
And just notice that all the functions

368
00:22:46,740 --> 00:22:50,860
are going through the data, but they're random

369
00:22:50,860 --> 00:22:52,040
away from the data.

370
00:22:55,380 --> 00:22:57,540
And then given that random function,

371
00:22:57,540 --> 00:23:00,140
you can ask a couple of questions.

372
00:23:00,140 --> 00:23:03,020
One is, what does the mean look like?

373
00:23:03,020 --> 00:23:05,580
And it's reasonable that the mean

374
00:23:05,580 --> 00:23:11,140
might be a lot smoother than all the random squiggly functions.

375
00:23:11,140 --> 00:23:13,660
And then you could ask, what is the standard deviation

376
00:23:13,660 --> 00:23:16,500
around the mean?

377
00:23:16,500 --> 00:23:18,100
And it's reasonable that it should

378
00:23:18,100 --> 00:23:23,760
grow as you move away from the data,

379
00:23:23,760 --> 00:23:27,120
and then shrink to 0 on the data.

380
00:23:27,120 --> 00:23:29,120
So that's one way it could look.

381
00:23:34,340 --> 00:23:35,900
So the point of a Gaussian process

382
00:23:35,900 --> 00:23:40,500
is it gives you a formula, just a simple thing that you

383
00:23:40,500 --> 00:23:41,840
can easily code up.

384
00:23:41,840 --> 00:23:44,820
It just involves some matrices and some linear algebra.

385
00:23:44,820 --> 00:23:51,040
But it gives you a formula, both for the mean function

386
00:23:51,040 --> 00:23:54,220
and the standard deviation.

387
00:23:54,220 --> 00:24:00,700
In other words, given a set of data, you've got these inputs,

388
00:24:00,700 --> 00:24:04,060
you can pick a new point on the x-axis.

389
00:24:04,060 --> 00:24:07,580
You say, I really care about this point on the x-axis.

390
00:24:07,580 --> 00:24:10,980
There's a formula that tells you the value of the mean

391
00:24:10,980 --> 00:24:15,560
and what the standard deviation is at that point.

392
00:24:15,560 --> 00:24:18,600
And then you can of sweep that point along the x-axis.

393
00:24:18,600 --> 00:24:21,340
And you can get this mean and you can

394
00:24:21,340 --> 00:24:23,500
get this standard deviation.

395
00:24:23,500 --> 00:24:25,860
So it basically gives you a formula

396
00:24:25,860 --> 00:24:27,460
for drawing this picture.

397
00:24:27,460 --> 00:24:29,740
That's one way to think simply about what

398
00:24:29,740 --> 00:24:31,740
a Gaussian process is.

399
00:24:31,740 --> 00:24:38,200
And basically, the shape of these standard deviations

400
00:24:38,200 --> 00:24:42,580
and what this picture looks like depends upon something

401
00:24:42,580 --> 00:24:45,020
that we call the covariance structure of the Gaussian

402
00:24:45,020 --> 00:24:45,520
process.

403
00:24:45,520 --> 00:24:50,300
And that's what we get to pick as machine learning people

404
00:24:50,300 --> 00:24:51,900
using Gaussian processes, is we get

405
00:24:51,900 --> 00:24:55,860
to pick the covariance structure.

406
00:24:55,860 --> 00:24:59,220
So that's the picture.

407
00:24:59,220 --> 00:25:05,420
Informally, I think this is probably the most useful--

408
00:25:05,420 --> 00:25:06,895
if you just want a quick takeaway,

409
00:25:06,895 --> 00:25:08,520
you don't want to go into all the math,

410
00:25:08,520 --> 00:25:11,820
this is quite useful understanding

411
00:25:11,820 --> 00:25:13,500
of what a Gaussian Process is.

412
00:25:13,500 --> 00:25:15,820
Just pretend that you don't know what

413
00:25:15,820 --> 00:25:17,280
a stochastic process is like.

414
00:25:17,280 --> 00:25:18,780
Most people honestly don't.

415
00:25:18,780 --> 00:25:21,140
But you do know what a random vector is.

416
00:25:21,140 --> 00:25:24,820
So let's just think about random vectors.

417
00:25:24,820 --> 00:25:29,490
So this is supposed to represent a vector, which I just

418
00:25:29,490 --> 00:25:33,290
turned horizontally so it would fit on the page.

419
00:25:33,290 --> 00:25:37,090
And imagine that I have a multivariate normal random

420
00:25:37,090 --> 00:25:41,930
vector with some covariance matrix and some mean,

421
00:25:41,930 --> 00:25:45,490
which is actually 0.

422
00:25:45,490 --> 00:25:50,330
Then I could plot each coordinate.

423
00:25:50,330 --> 00:25:52,930
I get one sample of this random vector,

424
00:25:52,930 --> 00:25:54,990
and I just plot it on an axis.

425
00:25:54,990 --> 00:26:00,090
So I just plot each entry.

426
00:26:00,090 --> 00:26:03,210
So this one corresponds to this one, this one corresponds

427
00:26:03,210 --> 00:26:04,510
to this one.

428
00:26:04,510 --> 00:26:09,130
And I could do that, and then I could just connect the dots.

429
00:26:09,130 --> 00:26:14,010
And I could call this somehow building

430
00:26:14,010 --> 00:26:19,210
a function from a random vector just by connecting the dots.

431
00:26:19,210 --> 00:26:21,210
So I could do that if I wanted to.

432
00:26:21,210 --> 00:26:26,190
Then I could say, in this picture,

433
00:26:26,190 --> 00:26:32,490
it's not that great because neighboring points on the x-axis

434
00:26:32,490 --> 00:26:35,730
are not correlated with each other.

435
00:26:35,730 --> 00:26:37,910
And usually if I have a nice function,

436
00:26:37,910 --> 00:26:41,050
I want nearby points to be similar to each other.

437
00:26:41,050 --> 00:26:43,090
So then you say, OK, hey, I'm just

438
00:26:43,090 --> 00:26:48,030
going to choose a covariance matrix with a special structure

439
00:26:48,030 --> 00:26:51,970
so that the covariance of neighboring

440
00:26:51,970 --> 00:26:57,870
entries in the vector are very correlated with each other.

441
00:26:57,870 --> 00:26:59,610
So I just pick the covariance such

442
00:26:59,610 --> 00:27:03,378
that neighboring entries in my vector are very correlated.

443
00:27:03,378 --> 00:27:05,670
Obviously, if I permuted all the entries in the vector,

444
00:27:05,670 --> 00:27:06,510
it would break that.

445
00:27:06,510 --> 00:27:07,990
But I'm not going to do that.

446
00:27:07,990 --> 00:27:11,090
So I'm basically going to pick a covariance structure such

447
00:27:11,090 --> 00:27:14,190
that neighboring entries are related.

448
00:27:14,190 --> 00:27:16,150
And then I would get a picture more like this,

449
00:27:16,150 --> 00:27:24,490
where when I connect the dots, the function,

450
00:27:24,490 --> 00:27:28,370
it looks more like a nice function that I like.

451
00:27:28,370 --> 00:27:29,930
And then basically a Gaussian process

452
00:27:29,930 --> 00:27:31,850
is exactly this construction, but then you

453
00:27:31,850 --> 00:27:36,070
let the number of grid cells in your vector,

454
00:27:36,070 --> 00:27:38,490
you let the dimension of your vector go to infinity.

455
00:27:38,490 --> 00:27:43,450
And basically, you pack these sample points

456
00:27:43,450 --> 00:27:45,710
more and more closely together, if that makes sense.

457
00:27:49,210 --> 00:27:51,810
Does the construction make sense?

458
00:27:51,810 --> 00:27:54,950
You're saying if you have a random vector,

459
00:27:54,950 --> 00:27:56,430
you can plot its coordinates.

460
00:27:56,430 --> 00:28:00,230
And then you can let the number of coordinates go to infinity,

461
00:28:00,230 --> 00:28:05,890
and it will just limit to something

462
00:28:05,890 --> 00:28:10,810
that's more like a continuous function available.

463
00:28:10,810 --> 00:28:11,890
Does that make sense?

464
00:28:11,890 --> 00:28:13,590
I've not really proved anything here.

465
00:28:13,590 --> 00:28:15,950
I've just given you an intuitive description.

466
00:28:18,810 --> 00:28:22,350
So if you forget everything else from the lecture,

467
00:28:22,350 --> 00:28:26,240
that's basically what a Gaussian process is.

468
00:28:26,240 --> 00:28:31,160
So now I want to give you a formal definition.

469
00:28:31,160 --> 00:28:33,760
So now we say that we have some input space

470
00:28:33,760 --> 00:28:42,080
X, which that was our x-axis in the previous slide.

471
00:28:42,080 --> 00:28:48,520
And then for every point in this input space,

472
00:28:48,520 --> 00:28:53,340
for infinitely many points, we let f of x be a random variable.

473
00:28:56,680 --> 00:29:01,920
So you think about the function f indexed by its argument x

474
00:29:01,920 --> 00:29:05,500
as being the continuous generalization

475
00:29:05,500 --> 00:29:13,840
of a random vector, where x is the index about which coordinate

476
00:29:13,840 --> 00:29:15,560
you are on on the vector.

477
00:29:15,560 --> 00:29:18,700
But that's just an informal thought.

478
00:29:21,240 --> 00:29:24,960
And then what we say is that because it's difficult to think

479
00:29:24,960 --> 00:29:28,120
about infinite dimensional objects,

480
00:29:28,120 --> 00:29:33,400
we say that if we choose a finite number of input points

481
00:29:33,400 --> 00:29:39,480
and inspect what the random object f looks like on any

482
00:29:39,480 --> 00:29:44,920
finite set of inputs-- so on any collection of inputs x1 to xn,

483
00:29:44,920 --> 00:29:47,950
where n is finite--

484
00:29:47,950 --> 00:29:50,900
we can consider that to be a random vector.

485
00:29:50,900 --> 00:29:52,360
Legitimately, it is a random vector

486
00:29:52,360 --> 00:29:54,600
because we're inspecting the function

487
00:29:54,600 --> 00:29:56,800
on a finite set of inputs.

488
00:29:56,800 --> 00:30:01,120
And if this random vector is Gaussian

489
00:30:01,120 --> 00:30:03,760
for any finite collection of inputs,

490
00:30:03,760 --> 00:30:06,440
then we would say that f is a Gaussian process.

491
00:30:06,440 --> 00:30:09,868
So this is the formal definition of the Gaussian process.

492
00:30:09,868 --> 00:30:12,160
It's kind of like an infinite dimensional object, where

493
00:30:12,160 --> 00:30:15,340
if you take any finite inspection of that object,

494
00:30:15,340 --> 00:30:17,960
it's a Gaussian vector, which is somehow

495
00:30:17,960 --> 00:30:20,580
related to what we were talking about on the previous slide.

496
00:30:25,060 --> 00:30:25,560
Yeah.

497
00:30:25,560 --> 00:30:26,060
Oh, yeah.

498
00:30:26,060 --> 00:30:26,760
Go ahead.

499
00:30:26,760 --> 00:30:31,560
AUDIENCE: Given a random vector, how would you verify

500
00:30:31,560 --> 00:30:34,960
whether or not it is Gaussian?

501
00:30:34,960 --> 00:30:37,960
JEREMY BERNSTEIN: It's more analytical.

502
00:30:37,960 --> 00:30:39,840
When we use this tool, it's more like you

503
00:30:39,840 --> 00:30:42,092
produce something which analytically, you know

504
00:30:42,092 --> 00:30:44,300
it satisfies this definition.

505
00:30:44,300 --> 00:30:46,080
You're very rarely in a situation

506
00:30:46,080 --> 00:30:52,700
where you would want to test empirically if it's a Gaussian.

507
00:30:52,700 --> 00:31:00,060
So you would have to verify it analytically in most cases.

508
00:31:00,060 --> 00:31:02,980
And I'm blanking on how exactly you would do that.

509
00:31:02,980 --> 00:31:06,440
But there are ways to do that.

510
00:31:06,440 --> 00:31:09,640
AUDIENCE: So you can define in the construction what

511
00:31:09,640 --> 00:31:11,860
the random variables are?

512
00:31:11,860 --> 00:31:15,443
What each function [INAUDIBLE], right?

513
00:31:15,443 --> 00:31:16,360
JEREMY BERNSTEIN: Yes.

514
00:31:16,360 --> 00:31:18,760
So if you're going to use Gaussian processes,

515
00:31:18,760 --> 00:31:21,860
you will build something which satisfies this definition.

516
00:31:21,860 --> 00:31:23,000
AUDIENCE: OK.

517
00:31:23,000 --> 00:31:25,280
JEREMY BERNSTEIN: And the thing is, what's difficult

518
00:31:25,280 --> 00:31:27,640
about this definition is it's not obvious that anything

519
00:31:27,640 --> 00:31:29,840
should satisfy this definition.

520
00:31:29,840 --> 00:31:33,160
And there's a theorem that most people who use

521
00:31:33,160 --> 00:31:36,700
Gaussian processes don't know.

522
00:31:36,700 --> 00:31:39,448
But I think it's called Kolmogorov extension

523
00:31:39,448 --> 00:31:40,740
theorem or something like this.

524
00:31:40,740 --> 00:31:42,800
And there's this area of math which

525
00:31:42,800 --> 00:31:46,440
is about proving that these things exist.

526
00:31:46,440 --> 00:31:48,000
But it turns out you actually don't

527
00:31:48,000 --> 00:31:52,253
need to know that area of math to actually use this stuff.

528
00:31:52,253 --> 00:31:53,920
AUDIENCE: And then I was just wondering,

529
00:31:53,920 --> 00:31:56,840
precisely what is the definition of a Gaussian vector?

530
00:31:56,840 --> 00:31:57,523
I can see from--

531
00:31:57,523 --> 00:31:58,440
JEREMY BERNSTEIN: Ooh!

532
00:31:58,440 --> 00:32:01,760
AUDIENCE: --the slide how you're just sampling from a Gaussian

533
00:32:01,760 --> 00:32:02,580
to construct it.

534
00:32:02,580 --> 00:32:03,420
But [INAUDIBLE].

535
00:32:03,420 --> 00:32:04,337
JEREMY BERNSTEIN: Yes.

536
00:32:04,337 --> 00:32:06,443
AUDIENCE: --want to know what it means.

537
00:32:06,443 --> 00:32:08,360
JEREMY BERNSTEIN: The simplest Gaussian vector

538
00:32:08,360 --> 00:32:11,980
is a Gaussian vector where the coordinates are iid.

539
00:32:11,980 --> 00:32:13,467
And then just for each coordinate,

540
00:32:13,467 --> 00:32:14,800
you sample a different Gaussian.

541
00:32:14,800 --> 00:32:16,860
And they're not related to each other.

542
00:32:16,860 --> 00:32:19,550
That's a simple one.

543
00:32:19,550 --> 00:32:23,550
And then a general one is defined with respect

544
00:32:23,550 --> 00:32:26,790
to a covariance matrix.

545
00:32:26,790 --> 00:32:30,750
And I'm probably going to butcher

546
00:32:30,750 --> 00:32:32,130
any explanation I give now.

547
00:32:32,130 --> 00:32:35,970
But I would just go on Wikipedia after the class.

548
00:32:39,790 --> 00:32:42,630
Another way to think about a general multivariate Gaussian

549
00:32:42,630 --> 00:32:46,870
is to do the iid coordinates and then rotate the space.

550
00:32:46,870 --> 00:32:48,890
And it turns out that's equivalent.

551
00:32:48,890 --> 00:32:52,110
I'm pretty sure that's basically equivalent to a general one,

552
00:32:52,110 --> 00:32:55,190
with rescaling the coordinates as well.

553
00:32:55,190 --> 00:32:56,550
Yeah?

554
00:32:56,550 --> 00:32:58,830
AUDIENCE: Is this just a fault of the dimensionality?

555
00:32:58,830 --> 00:33:01,030
Or if it's spaced within [INAUDIBLE] Gaussian lives

556
00:33:01,030 --> 00:33:03,870
would be the number of data points, right?

557
00:33:03,870 --> 00:33:05,630
[INAUDIBLE]?

558
00:33:05,630 --> 00:33:07,650
JEREMY BERNSTEIN: Yeah, but for this definition,

559
00:33:07,650 --> 00:33:12,090
in this case, that would be if I had a finite thing.

560
00:33:12,090 --> 00:33:17,670
But this definition is for any collection of n for any n.

561
00:33:17,670 --> 00:33:21,730
So it has to hold for all possible collections of inputs.

562
00:33:21,730 --> 00:33:23,970
It's quite a technical definition.

563
00:33:27,750 --> 00:33:30,830
Sorry, is that clear?

564
00:33:30,830 --> 00:33:32,670
AUDIENCE: Yeah, I guess just initially when

565
00:33:32,670 --> 00:33:35,995
I first thought Gaussian, I just imagined the Gaussian curves.

566
00:33:35,995 --> 00:33:37,370
So I imagine that being Gaussian.

567
00:33:37,370 --> 00:33:39,750
But obviously that's not what this is.

568
00:33:39,750 --> 00:33:41,730
And then I thought maybe [INAUDIBLE].

569
00:33:41,730 --> 00:33:43,030
But it looks like--

570
00:33:43,030 --> 00:33:43,870
yeah.

571
00:33:43,870 --> 00:33:45,443
I understand better now.

572
00:33:45,443 --> 00:33:47,110
JEREMY BERNSTEIN: Yeah, it's conditioned

573
00:33:47,110 --> 00:33:48,678
on having a set of inputs.

574
00:33:48,678 --> 00:33:50,470
AUDIENCE: I'm thinking of a neural network,

575
00:33:50,470 --> 00:33:53,830
and it's defined on some data points.

576
00:33:53,830 --> 00:33:59,270
That's Gaussian in the sense that the function that it's

577
00:33:59,270 --> 00:34:02,910
applying on the data points in the dimension of the space,

578
00:34:02,910 --> 00:34:06,310
the dimensionality of the data points for, I guess,

579
00:34:06,310 --> 00:34:08,870
larger is Gaussian.

580
00:34:08,870 --> 00:34:11,030
I guess I'm just trying to reconcile this

581
00:34:11,030 --> 00:34:12,832
with the neural network not being--

582
00:34:12,832 --> 00:34:13,790
JEREMY BERNSTEIN: Yeah.

583
00:34:13,790 --> 00:34:15,030
AUDIENCE: [INAUDIBLE]

584
00:34:15,030 --> 00:34:15,909
JEREMY BERNSTEIN: OK.

585
00:34:15,909 --> 00:34:19,350
AUDIENCE: I'm thinking I'm close, but [INAUDIBLE].

586
00:34:19,350 --> 00:34:22,070
JEREMY BERNSTEIN: I have a concrete example.

587
00:34:22,070 --> 00:34:25,790
I'm just not sure how much further into the lecture

588
00:34:25,790 --> 00:34:28,230
it is going to be.

589
00:34:28,230 --> 00:34:30,969
But I think it's going to resolve this question.

590
00:34:30,969 --> 00:34:36,259
So let me just plow on, but keep the question.

591
00:34:36,259 --> 00:34:39,730
But I just have a few more high-level things to say.

592
00:34:39,730 --> 00:34:42,610
And then, oh, yeah, I have a picture as well.

593
00:34:42,610 --> 00:34:45,097
So let me just go on.

594
00:34:45,097 --> 00:34:46,889
I think I'm going to resolve your question.

595
00:34:50,429 --> 00:34:54,190
So I should have defined what a standard multivariate

596
00:34:54,190 --> 00:34:55,010
Gaussian is.

597
00:34:55,010 --> 00:34:59,550
And I'll add that for next time.

598
00:34:59,550 --> 00:35:02,750
So this is just to say that a Gaussian process is

599
00:35:02,750 --> 00:35:05,470
the thing that generalizes a finite-dimensional Gaussian

600
00:35:05,470 --> 00:35:12,310
vector to somehow an infinite-dimensional random

601
00:35:12,310 --> 00:35:13,210
function.

602
00:35:17,590 --> 00:35:21,270
And if a Gaussian random vector is described

603
00:35:21,270 --> 00:35:24,990
by a covariance matrix, well, actually this thing

604
00:35:24,990 --> 00:35:28,390
that we call a Gaussian process is

605
00:35:28,390 --> 00:35:29,870
going to be described by something

606
00:35:29,870 --> 00:35:35,020
that we call the covariance function, which tells us--

607
00:35:35,020 --> 00:35:38,710
at two different inputs, what is the covariance

608
00:35:38,710 --> 00:35:41,190
between the function at those inputs.

609
00:35:41,190 --> 00:35:47,270
So that's how the covariance matrix gets generalized.

610
00:35:47,270 --> 00:35:54,330
And an example, if in the finite case,

611
00:35:54,330 --> 00:36:02,310
the covariance was e to the i minus j squared, then

612
00:36:02,310 --> 00:36:06,390
here in the continuous case, it would be something like e

613
00:36:06,390 --> 00:36:11,090
to the x minus x prime squared.

614
00:36:11,090 --> 00:36:13,360
So that's how that would generalize.

615
00:36:17,940 --> 00:36:24,500
So hopefully this slide will help build some more intuition

616
00:36:24,500 --> 00:36:26,200
for what we're talking about.

617
00:36:26,200 --> 00:36:27,000
OK, go ahead.

618
00:36:27,000 --> 00:36:29,380
AUDIENCE: [INAUDIBLE] dimensional function,

619
00:36:29,380 --> 00:36:31,300
you just need a function that accepts

620
00:36:31,300 --> 00:36:32,697
an infinite number of inputs?

621
00:36:32,697 --> 00:36:33,780
JEREMY BERNSTEIN: Exactly.

622
00:36:33,780 --> 00:36:34,917
AUDIENCE: OK.

623
00:36:34,917 --> 00:36:36,000
JEREMY BERNSTEIN: Exactly.

624
00:36:45,460 --> 00:36:47,020
Let me just say again that, yeah,

625
00:36:47,020 --> 00:36:52,340
exactly, we've got the x-axis or the real line

626
00:36:52,340 --> 00:36:53,400
as our input space.

627
00:36:53,400 --> 00:36:55,660
So there's an infinite number of things on it.

628
00:36:55,660 --> 00:37:00,700
And here we're going to consider two examples, x very close to x

629
00:37:00,700 --> 00:37:05,780
prime, which is this picture.

630
00:37:05,780 --> 00:37:13,220
And basically, we're going to choose the covariance function

631
00:37:13,220 --> 00:37:15,680
almost always in our Gaussian process,

632
00:37:15,680 --> 00:37:20,540
such that if I sample the random functions

633
00:37:20,540 --> 00:37:22,960
and I plot a scatter plot--

634
00:37:22,960 --> 00:37:26,940
so I sample one random function and I get this point

635
00:37:26,940 --> 00:37:28,923
for f of x and f of x prime.

636
00:37:28,923 --> 00:37:30,840
And then I sample a different random function,

637
00:37:30,840 --> 00:37:32,100
and I get this point.

638
00:37:32,100 --> 00:37:34,020
And I sample a different one.

639
00:37:34,020 --> 00:37:37,380
But because we're imagining that x and x prime are close

640
00:37:37,380 --> 00:37:43,620
together, the scatter plot that I get is highly correlated.

641
00:37:43,620 --> 00:37:46,560
And as x and x prime get closer and closer together,

642
00:37:46,560 --> 00:37:51,660
it should concentrate on the samples being the same.

643
00:37:51,660 --> 00:37:57,500
But then as I move the point x prime further away,

644
00:37:57,500 --> 00:38:02,220
now I move x prime over here so x and x prime are kind of far

645
00:38:02,220 --> 00:38:05,940
apart, the function values that I

646
00:38:05,940 --> 00:38:09,420
get from sampling the functions are now uncorrelated

647
00:38:09,420 --> 00:38:15,620
with each other, if it's a Gaussian process with this kind

648
00:38:15,620 --> 00:38:18,200
of covariate structure, which is usually what you have.

649
00:38:22,980 --> 00:38:24,940
And basically, it's the choice of what

650
00:38:24,940 --> 00:38:26,980
we call the covariance function that actually

651
00:38:26,980 --> 00:38:30,860
encodes what we mean by nearby.

652
00:38:30,860 --> 00:38:33,160
And for different choices of covariance function,

653
00:38:33,160 --> 00:38:36,340
that could mean something else.

654
00:38:36,340 --> 00:38:38,300
And just to give you some common examples

655
00:38:38,300 --> 00:38:40,940
of covariance functions, one would

656
00:38:40,940 --> 00:38:47,220
be to take e to the negative squared distance between the two

657
00:38:47,220 --> 00:38:49,180
inputs.

658
00:38:49,180 --> 00:38:51,560
And another, if we have a multivariate case,

659
00:38:51,560 --> 00:38:55,700
it could be to take some kind of dot product between the inputs.

660
00:38:55,700 --> 00:38:58,700
Basically, the covariance structure

661
00:38:58,700 --> 00:39:01,640
is some kind of measure of similarity between the inputs.

662
00:39:01,640 --> 00:39:06,420
And we use that to induce what the correlation structure looks

663
00:39:06,420 --> 00:39:08,040
like between different inputs.

664
00:39:15,500 --> 00:39:16,500
Yeah, go ahead.

665
00:39:16,500 --> 00:39:18,540
AUDIENCE: Does this work with language modeling

666
00:39:18,540 --> 00:39:20,740
when you're learning an embedding?

667
00:39:20,740 --> 00:39:23,788
Because I guess the words are not continuous, right?

668
00:39:23,788 --> 00:39:25,080
You can't take the dot product.

669
00:39:25,080 --> 00:39:27,143
But we have an embedding that's learned.

670
00:39:27,143 --> 00:39:28,060
JEREMY BERNSTEIN: Yes.

671
00:39:28,060 --> 00:39:31,580
Interestingly, the covariance function--

672
00:39:31,580 --> 00:39:34,020
yeah, if you have discrete inputs--

673
00:39:36,860 --> 00:39:39,460
there are ways to define covariance functions,

674
00:39:39,460 --> 00:39:42,380
even on weird spaces.

675
00:39:42,380 --> 00:39:45,180
The inputs don't have to be the real line.

676
00:39:45,180 --> 00:39:46,980
The inputs to the covariance function

677
00:39:46,980 --> 00:39:51,300
can be any inputs that--

678
00:39:51,300 --> 00:39:53,700
so you can extend this construction

679
00:39:53,700 --> 00:39:56,272
to weird input spaces.

680
00:39:56,272 --> 00:39:57,980
That was one of the reasons people really

681
00:39:57,980 --> 00:40:00,860
like kernel methods as well, because they

682
00:40:00,860 --> 00:40:02,380
have that same property.

683
00:40:02,380 --> 00:40:05,980
In some sense, a downside of neural nets

684
00:40:05,980 --> 00:40:12,732
is it's not obvious how to apply it to language, let's say.

685
00:40:12,732 --> 00:40:14,190
OK, we came up with a way to do it.

686
00:40:14,190 --> 00:40:17,310
We just embed all the inputs and then learn the embeddings.

687
00:40:17,310 --> 00:40:19,050
But it wasn't obvious that that was

688
00:40:19,050 --> 00:40:21,270
going to work before people showed that it worked.

689
00:40:21,270 --> 00:40:23,330
So people came up with alternatives.

690
00:40:23,330 --> 00:40:28,210
And this is compatible with different input spaces,

691
00:40:28,210 --> 00:40:29,475
basically.

692
00:40:29,475 --> 00:40:30,850
AUDIENCE: But I guess, if we were

693
00:40:30,850 --> 00:40:36,290
to say that an LLM was a Gaussian process or something,

694
00:40:36,290 --> 00:40:40,010
it would mean that whatever the solutions it finds

695
00:40:40,010 --> 00:40:43,550
are both a Gaussian process in terms of embeddings,

696
00:40:43,550 --> 00:40:46,250
and that it's also figuring out the right embeddings

697
00:40:46,250 --> 00:40:48,650
for the solutions of the Gaussian process.

698
00:40:48,650 --> 00:40:51,450
Because the embeddings are [INAUDIBLE].

699
00:40:51,450 --> 00:40:54,843
JEREMY BERNSTEIN: So I haven't got to what I precisely

700
00:40:54,843 --> 00:40:57,010
mean when I say that we can think of neural networks

701
00:40:57,010 --> 00:40:58,030
as Gaussian processes.

702
00:40:58,030 --> 00:41:01,870
So let's wait to get there and then discuss this a bit more.

703
00:41:12,990 --> 00:41:15,152
This is the last abstract slide.

704
00:41:15,152 --> 00:41:16,610
And then I'm going to start talking

705
00:41:16,610 --> 00:41:19,590
about a more concrete example for a neural network.

706
00:41:19,590 --> 00:41:25,817
So I hope that will make things a little clearer.

707
00:41:25,817 --> 00:41:27,650
But the last thing that I want to talk about

708
00:41:27,650 --> 00:41:31,330
is how, if you're doing machine learning with a Gaussian

709
00:41:31,330 --> 00:41:34,850
process, how you actually make predictions-- or some people

710
00:41:34,850 --> 00:41:40,230
would say, how you do inference, in machine learning speak.

711
00:41:40,230 --> 00:41:44,210
But basically, if you've got training data or you've

712
00:41:44,210 --> 00:41:47,770
got inputs and you want to make a prediction at a point

713
00:41:47,770 --> 00:41:50,510
that you haven't seen, how do you actually do that?

714
00:41:54,170 --> 00:42:01,210
And roughly, the idea is that if you're given inputs x1 to xn

715
00:42:01,210 --> 00:42:06,670
and you know the function values f of x1 up to f of xn--

716
00:42:06,670 --> 00:42:09,930
so that's your input data, is the inputs and the function

717
00:42:09,930 --> 00:42:11,610
values.

718
00:42:11,610 --> 00:42:15,730
We want to predict f of x-star, where x-star is a new test

719
00:42:15,730 --> 00:42:17,270
point, a new test input.

720
00:42:17,270 --> 00:42:20,450
That's what we want to predict.

721
00:42:20,450 --> 00:42:30,890
You just think about stacking all of the things you know

722
00:42:30,890 --> 00:42:36,410
and the thing that you don't know into one big vector.

723
00:42:36,410 --> 00:42:38,090
Because it's a Gaussian process, we

724
00:42:38,090 --> 00:42:42,050
know that the joint distribution of this quantity

725
00:42:42,050 --> 00:42:46,510
is Gaussian, because that's the definition of a Gaussian

726
00:42:46,510 --> 00:42:47,010
process.

727
00:42:47,010 --> 00:42:52,650
For any collection of inputs, that includes the n

728
00:42:52,650 --> 00:42:55,565
inputs that I was given and the one that I want to predict on,

729
00:42:55,565 --> 00:42:57,690
we know that the joint distribution of those things

730
00:42:57,690 --> 00:43:00,050
is a multivariate Gaussian just by the definition

731
00:43:00,050 --> 00:43:01,550
of the Gaussian process.

732
00:43:01,550 --> 00:43:03,050
And then you can say that, hey, if I

733
00:43:03,050 --> 00:43:05,490
have a multivariate Gaussian vector

734
00:43:05,490 --> 00:43:08,730
and I know its distribution, one of the standard things that that

735
00:43:08,730 --> 00:43:12,290
tells you how to do is, given the first n coordinates,

736
00:43:12,290 --> 00:43:13,890
what is the conditional distribution

737
00:43:13,890 --> 00:43:15,190
of the last coordinate?

738
00:43:15,190 --> 00:43:17,530
So that's a standard manipulation

739
00:43:17,530 --> 00:43:20,570
that you can do with Gaussian vectors is just,

740
00:43:20,570 --> 00:43:22,130
what is the conditional distribution

741
00:43:22,130 --> 00:43:26,250
of the last coordinate on all the previous ones?

742
00:43:26,250 --> 00:43:27,950
So there's just a formula for that.

743
00:43:27,950 --> 00:43:31,370
It's just called conditioning with Gaussian vectors

744
00:43:31,370 --> 00:43:32,650
or something like that.

745
00:43:32,650 --> 00:43:36,890
And that is precisely the formula that

746
00:43:36,890 --> 00:43:38,930
gives you this kind of picture.

747
00:43:38,930 --> 00:43:41,010
Because basically, you take this point x-star

748
00:43:41,010 --> 00:43:43,850
and you sweep it along this x-axis.

749
00:43:43,850 --> 00:43:51,290
And the formula tells you both what the mean of this coordinate

750
00:43:51,290 --> 00:43:53,270
is conditioned on these ones.

751
00:43:53,270 --> 00:43:55,810
And it also tells you what the standard deviation is.

752
00:43:55,810 --> 00:43:59,390
And interestingly, because Gaussians are so well-behaved,

753
00:43:59,390 --> 00:44:02,440
the conditional distribution of the last coordinate conditioned

754
00:44:02,440 --> 00:44:06,180
on all the early ones is itself a Gaussian distribution.

755
00:44:06,180 --> 00:44:11,720
So if you take a Gaussian vector and you condition on the first n

756
00:44:11,720 --> 00:44:16,640
coordinates, the distribution of the last one is Gaussian.

757
00:44:16,640 --> 00:44:19,340
So in particular, there's just a formula for this mean.

758
00:44:19,340 --> 00:44:21,820
And there's a formula for this standard deviation.

759
00:44:21,820 --> 00:44:25,000
And that's a closed-form expression just involving

760
00:44:25,000 --> 00:44:28,840
evaluating the covariance matrix, basically, on the input

761
00:44:28,840 --> 00:44:30,840
points.

762
00:44:30,840 --> 00:44:33,720
So I realized I'm not really telling you explicitly what

763
00:44:33,720 --> 00:44:35,600
these formulae are, but there are things

764
00:44:35,600 --> 00:44:37,000
that you can just look at.

765
00:44:37,000 --> 00:44:38,993
And I honestly think if you haven't seen this

766
00:44:38,993 --> 00:44:41,160
before, it's maybe easier just to have the intuitive

767
00:44:41,160 --> 00:44:41,720
explanation.

768
00:44:41,720 --> 00:44:44,320
And then you can just look up what the formulae

769
00:44:44,320 --> 00:44:45,660
are if you're interested.

770
00:44:56,280 --> 00:44:59,040
Now I want to tell you what this has

771
00:44:59,040 --> 00:45:02,160
to do with neural networks, which

772
00:45:02,160 --> 00:45:04,740
was kind of the point of introducing this stuff.

773
00:45:07,920 --> 00:45:11,240
And that's a result called the Neural Network Gaussian Process

774
00:45:11,240 --> 00:45:14,160
Correspondence, which is something that a lot of people

775
00:45:14,160 --> 00:45:16,380
have been really excited about over the years.

776
00:45:20,360 --> 00:45:24,640
So now forget everything we just talked about and just think

777
00:45:24,640 --> 00:45:26,080
about a neural network.

778
00:45:26,080 --> 00:45:30,840
And we just think the simplest case is arguably just

779
00:45:30,840 --> 00:45:32,560
multilayer perceptron.

780
00:45:32,560 --> 00:45:41,760
So we've got weight matrices, W1, W2, W3, up to WL.

781
00:45:41,760 --> 00:45:47,880
And what we can do is we can just sample the weight matrices

782
00:45:47,880 --> 00:45:48,780
randomly.

783
00:45:48,780 --> 00:45:50,100
And it gives me the function.

784
00:45:50,100 --> 00:45:52,320
The neural network gives me a function.

785
00:45:52,320 --> 00:45:54,120
I write that function down.

786
00:45:54,120 --> 00:45:56,580
And then I resample the weight matrices.

787
00:45:56,580 --> 00:45:58,380
And I get a new random function.

788
00:45:58,380 --> 00:46:00,020
And I write that function down.

789
00:46:00,020 --> 00:46:02,720
And I just keep sampling the weight matrices over and over

790
00:46:02,720 --> 00:46:03,220
again.

791
00:46:03,220 --> 00:46:05,740
And it's going to give me a distribution over functions.

792
00:46:05,740 --> 00:46:08,260
And I can ask, what is that distribution?

793
00:46:08,260 --> 00:46:09,740
What properties does it have?

794
00:46:15,920 --> 00:46:24,240
So let's pretend that we did that, and we generated,

795
00:46:24,240 --> 00:46:30,000
in this case, 1,000 random functions by sampling 1,000 set

796
00:46:30,000 --> 00:46:33,120
of weights for my neural net.

797
00:46:33,120 --> 00:46:36,400
So I re-initialize my neural network 1,000 times.

798
00:46:36,400 --> 00:46:38,760
And I want to inspect the functions.

799
00:46:38,760 --> 00:46:41,040
So the way I decide to do that is I'm

800
00:46:41,040 --> 00:46:44,880
going to pick two inputs, x and x-prime.

801
00:46:44,880 --> 00:46:49,280
And in general, x could be a picture of a cat

802
00:46:49,280 --> 00:46:51,520
and x-prime could be a picture of a dog.

803
00:46:51,520 --> 00:46:53,940
But it's just two inputs.

804
00:46:53,940 --> 00:46:55,680
And I fixed them.

805
00:46:55,680 --> 00:47:08,400
And then I get my 1,000 functions, f1 up to f1000.

806
00:47:08,400 --> 00:47:15,280
And for those two inputs, I just stack

807
00:47:15,280 --> 00:47:16,465
the outputs of the network.

808
00:47:16,465 --> 00:47:18,840
So let's just imagine that the output is one dimensional,

809
00:47:18,840 --> 00:47:20,160
for simplicity.

810
00:47:20,160 --> 00:47:28,680
And I just build a 2 by 1,000 matrix of all the outputs

811
00:47:28,680 --> 00:47:30,880
of my random networks.

812
00:47:30,880 --> 00:47:32,980
And I'm just going to plot them on a scatter plot

813
00:47:32,980 --> 00:47:35,640
and just see what it looks like.

814
00:47:35,640 --> 00:47:41,600
And I actually did this, but I did it two years ago.

815
00:47:41,600 --> 00:47:43,320
But I did it.

816
00:47:43,320 --> 00:47:49,280
This is a truck that's in the CIFAR-10 dataset.

817
00:47:49,280 --> 00:47:51,340
And this was a three-layer MLP.

818
00:47:51,340 --> 00:47:54,200
And the width of the network, the width of the hidden layer

819
00:47:54,200 --> 00:47:57,470
was 1,000.

820
00:47:57,470 --> 00:48:05,710
And I did it for two pairs of inputs, so input 1 and input 2.

821
00:48:05,710 --> 00:48:08,510
So input first of all, and then second, I

822
00:48:08,510 --> 00:48:10,370
did it for input 1 and input 3.

823
00:48:14,210 --> 00:48:16,930
So input 2 is actually just input 1.

824
00:48:16,930 --> 00:48:18,830
It's the same truck, but with a little bit

825
00:48:18,830 --> 00:48:20,310
of jitter in the pixels.

826
00:48:20,310 --> 00:48:22,990
I just added a little bit of noise to the pixels.

827
00:48:22,990 --> 00:48:25,130
And then input 3, it's the same truck,

828
00:48:25,130 --> 00:48:29,590
but I just added a lot more noise to the pixels,

829
00:48:29,590 --> 00:48:33,110
just to show the difference.

830
00:48:33,110 --> 00:48:37,950
And then for my 1,000 random networks,

831
00:48:37,950 --> 00:48:43,910
this is what the network outputs look like for the first pair.

832
00:48:43,910 --> 00:48:46,830
And then this is what they look like for the second pair.

833
00:48:46,830 --> 00:48:54,470
So the interesting thing is, it kind of looks like the thing

834
00:48:54,470 --> 00:48:57,750
that we were talking about, the Gaussian process thing,

835
00:48:57,750 --> 00:49:01,330
where the two inputs, which are kind of similar to each other,

836
00:49:01,330 --> 00:49:04,990
seem to have more correlated random outputs,

837
00:49:04,990 --> 00:49:08,550
whereas the two inputs that are a bit less

838
00:49:08,550 --> 00:49:10,270
similar to each other, the outputs

839
00:49:10,270 --> 00:49:12,090
are slightly less correlated.

840
00:49:17,950 --> 00:49:23,590
And then this is displaying it for pairs of inputs.

841
00:49:23,590 --> 00:49:25,950
But instead of having two inputs,

842
00:49:25,950 --> 00:49:31,390
I could make this same table, but I could put five inputs

843
00:49:31,390 --> 00:49:34,310
and then sample 1,000 networks and then plot that

844
00:49:34,310 --> 00:49:36,270
five-dimensional scatter plot.

845
00:49:36,270 --> 00:49:39,830
And then I could ask, does that thing look Gaussian?

846
00:49:39,830 --> 00:49:43,990
And my claim is it will because of this Neural Network Gaussian

847
00:49:43,990 --> 00:49:46,150
Process Correspondence.

848
00:49:46,150 --> 00:49:48,090
For a sufficiently wide neural network,

849
00:49:48,090 --> 00:49:50,850
these scatter plots actually are Gaussian.

850
00:49:50,850 --> 00:49:55,650
And we would actually have to do what Zach was saying.

851
00:49:55,650 --> 00:49:58,790
It's like, how do we actually know these are Gaussian?

852
00:49:58,790 --> 00:50:00,390
That's a good question.

853
00:50:00,390 --> 00:50:01,130
I don't know.

854
00:50:01,130 --> 00:50:03,390
There's tests for Gaussianity that if you

855
00:50:03,390 --> 00:50:06,330
wanted to run on this scatter plot and you could do that test.

856
00:50:06,330 --> 00:50:08,250
But I don't know how to do that.

857
00:50:08,250 --> 00:50:10,530
But my claim is that really they are Gaussian.

858
00:50:10,530 --> 00:50:14,830
And it's because the neural network is a wide network

859
00:50:14,830 --> 00:50:17,330
that it has to be the case.

860
00:50:25,870 --> 00:50:32,630
So what is the formal claim of the correspondence?

861
00:50:32,630 --> 00:50:38,710
It's something like this, that if we take a neural network

862
00:50:38,710 --> 00:50:42,030
and we randomly sample the weights, then

863
00:50:42,030 --> 00:50:45,330
so long as the network was very, very, very wide--

864
00:50:49,630 --> 00:50:55,420
if I pick any finite collection of inputs--

865
00:50:55,420 --> 00:50:58,540
basically for any finite collection of inputs,

866
00:50:58,540 --> 00:51:00,900
the distribution of outputs is actually

867
00:51:00,900 --> 00:51:08,620
a multivariate Gaussian, which is the definition of a Gaussian

868
00:51:08,620 --> 00:51:09,160
process.

869
00:51:09,160 --> 00:51:10,240
That was the definition.

870
00:51:10,240 --> 00:51:13,080
If you have that for any finite collection of inputs,

871
00:51:13,080 --> 00:51:14,960
the distribution of outputs is Gaussian,

872
00:51:14,960 --> 00:51:18,220
then that's satisfying the definition of being a Gaussian

873
00:51:18,220 --> 00:51:19,340
process.

874
00:51:19,340 --> 00:51:21,720
And it also would explain these pictures.

875
00:51:24,900 --> 00:51:29,180
And importantly, what the covariance function is

876
00:51:29,180 --> 00:51:33,180
depends on things like what the architecture of the network is

877
00:51:33,180 --> 00:51:36,140
and what the nonlinearity is.

878
00:51:36,140 --> 00:51:39,780
So it's an interesting result where,

879
00:51:39,780 --> 00:51:43,440
given a particular network architecture,

880
00:51:43,440 --> 00:51:47,180
you can expand its width and there

881
00:51:47,180 --> 00:51:50,900
will be a corresponding covariance kernel or covariance

882
00:51:50,900 --> 00:51:58,220
function that is basically a function of what the network

883
00:51:58,220 --> 00:51:59,360
architecture is.

884
00:52:02,820 --> 00:52:04,820
And that's what I meant at the beginning

885
00:52:04,820 --> 00:52:08,980
when I said that actually the architecture somehow

886
00:52:08,980 --> 00:52:12,260
has an opinion built into the choice of architecture

887
00:52:12,260 --> 00:52:14,520
about what data points are similar to each other,

888
00:52:14,520 --> 00:52:16,880
and which data points are dissimilar to each other.

889
00:52:16,880 --> 00:52:19,380
And one way to extract that information

890
00:52:19,380 --> 00:52:22,320
is to take the architecture to be really, really wide.

891
00:52:22,320 --> 00:52:24,140
And you extract this kernel function

892
00:52:24,140 --> 00:52:31,540
or this covariance function, so this function of the form sigma;

893
00:52:31,540 --> 00:52:33,300
x, x-prime.

894
00:52:33,300 --> 00:52:34,723
And maybe sigma depends on things

895
00:52:34,723 --> 00:52:36,140
like what the depth of the network

896
00:52:36,140 --> 00:52:38,480
is, what the nonlinearity is, and so on.

897
00:52:48,500 --> 00:52:51,007
AUDIENCE: So I guess in terms of practicality,

898
00:52:51,007 --> 00:52:52,840
because we're making it really, really wide,

899
00:52:52,840 --> 00:52:55,843
we're really giving it a good opportunity to overfit.

900
00:52:55,843 --> 00:52:58,260
But I guess the idea is because we're taking a random look

901
00:52:58,260 --> 00:53:01,540
at overfitting, that's fine?

902
00:53:01,540 --> 00:53:03,260
That's OK?

903
00:53:03,260 --> 00:53:06,860
JEREMY BERNSTEIN: Yeah, that's a really interesting question.

904
00:53:06,860 --> 00:53:08,900
Let me make some space.

905
00:53:08,900 --> 00:53:11,525
Also, I think this lecture is on the shorter side.

906
00:53:11,525 --> 00:53:12,900
So the more interesting questions

907
00:53:12,900 --> 00:53:18,060
you ask, then the more learning there can be or something.

908
00:53:23,620 --> 00:53:27,020
So the question is, if the neural network is really, really

909
00:53:27,020 --> 00:53:29,060
wide, then intuitively I would think

910
00:53:29,060 --> 00:53:31,180
that it's going to somehow overfit because it has

911
00:53:31,180 --> 00:53:34,760
too much capacity for my data.

912
00:53:38,020 --> 00:53:42,940
But the interesting thing about the Gaussian process picture

913
00:53:42,940 --> 00:53:47,540
is that, let's say that we have some data

914
00:53:47,540 --> 00:53:50,540
and we get this distribution of functions.

915
00:53:50,540 --> 00:53:55,160
One interesting function could be the mean function.

916
00:53:55,160 --> 00:53:57,640
So remember that not only is there the mean,

917
00:53:57,640 --> 00:54:01,320
but these functions are going through the data.

918
00:54:05,900 --> 00:54:09,980
And because it's random and because it's Gaussian,

919
00:54:09,980 --> 00:54:12,580
there's some probability of getting a really horrible

920
00:54:12,580 --> 00:54:15,300
function, really bizarre.

921
00:54:15,300 --> 00:54:20,120
One function could actually do something like,

922
00:54:20,120 --> 00:54:22,620
it goes through this data point and then it goes all the way

923
00:54:22,620 --> 00:54:24,640
up here, and then it comes all the way back down,

924
00:54:24,640 --> 00:54:25,960
and then it goes all the way back up.

925
00:54:25,960 --> 00:54:28,320
And there's some probability of getting that function.

926
00:54:28,320 --> 00:54:31,940
But interestingly, it's a very, very small

927
00:54:31,940 --> 00:54:36,500
because things can't deviate too much

928
00:54:36,500 --> 00:54:40,340
from the mean with very significant probability

929
00:54:40,340 --> 00:54:41,780
basically.

930
00:54:41,780 --> 00:54:44,720
And even if they do deviate a lot from the mean--

931
00:54:44,720 --> 00:54:46,440
let's say the variance is really high--

932
00:54:50,210 --> 00:54:51,730
well, I can always try to extract

933
00:54:51,730 --> 00:54:53,772
what the mean is, because I know that the mean is

934
00:54:53,772 --> 00:54:54,950
a nice, smooth function.

935
00:54:54,950 --> 00:54:57,230
So in the context of this correspondence,

936
00:54:57,230 --> 00:54:58,790
it's an interesting question to ask--

937
00:55:03,010 --> 00:55:04,930
how can I get the distribution of functions

938
00:55:04,930 --> 00:55:07,250
to actually concentrate on the mean?

939
00:55:07,250 --> 00:55:10,010
In other words, what do I need to do to the neural network

940
00:55:10,010 --> 00:55:13,880
to get this standard deviation to go to 0,

941
00:55:13,880 --> 00:55:15,630
relative to the mean, if that makes sense?

942
00:55:15,630 --> 00:55:16,870
Is there something I can do?

943
00:55:16,870 --> 00:55:18,453
And I thought about that at one point.

944
00:55:18,453 --> 00:55:23,770
And the argument is that if you make the standard deviation

945
00:55:23,770 --> 00:55:26,770
that you sample the weights from very, very small, then

946
00:55:26,770 --> 00:55:29,090
it actually causes the distribution

947
00:55:29,090 --> 00:55:30,830
to collapse onto the mean.

948
00:55:30,830 --> 00:55:33,470
And then with very high probability,

949
00:55:33,470 --> 00:55:36,810
all the samples are very close to the mean.

950
00:55:36,810 --> 00:55:39,610
But I didn't want to dwell.

951
00:55:39,610 --> 00:55:42,068
Yeah, it's something to think about.

952
00:55:42,068 --> 00:55:42,610
AUDIENCE: OK.

953
00:55:46,952 --> 00:55:48,410
JEREMY BERNSTEIN: Oh, yeah, there's

954
00:55:48,410 --> 00:55:53,250
another answer to that question, which is it's basically like--

955
00:55:53,250 --> 00:55:55,250
I'm not sure if Phillip talked about that

956
00:55:55,250 --> 00:55:58,330
in one of his lectures-- but the idea of neural networks

957
00:55:58,330 --> 00:56:00,190
being very, very overparameterized,

958
00:56:00,190 --> 00:56:04,390
but they can still represent lots of nice, simple functions.

959
00:56:04,390 --> 00:56:06,970
So the fact that the network is very, very wide

960
00:56:06,970 --> 00:56:09,830
does not imply that you're going to overfit.

961
00:56:09,830 --> 00:56:12,270
It just implies that it's possible to overfit.

962
00:56:18,520 --> 00:56:19,810
Yeah, go ahead.

963
00:56:19,810 --> 00:56:21,930
AUDIENCE: Why does that require you

964
00:56:21,930 --> 00:56:24,930
to have standard weights for the neural network?

965
00:56:24,930 --> 00:56:31,850
Also, for example, if you sample 1,000 random network

966
00:56:31,850 --> 00:56:38,610
and initially the network is a random Gaussian distribution

967
00:56:38,610 --> 00:56:39,810
by sampling it.

968
00:56:39,810 --> 00:56:43,030
And [INAUDIBLE] you draw the plot.

969
00:56:43,030 --> 00:56:47,170
But the output, you still can't say it follows the Gaussian

970
00:56:47,170 --> 00:56:48,030
distribution.

971
00:56:48,030 --> 00:56:50,690
So I'm just wondering, it seems like you

972
00:56:50,690 --> 00:56:56,690
can do the same thing in your network for the kernel methods.

973
00:56:56,690 --> 00:57:00,690
You can learn the sufficient statistic, like the mean value

974
00:57:00,690 --> 00:57:05,970
and sigma value, without a large hyperparameter tuning

975
00:57:05,970 --> 00:57:08,650
or without large tuning, how can you

976
00:57:08,650 --> 00:57:12,170
learn a sufficient statistic?

977
00:57:12,170 --> 00:57:13,930
JEREMY BERNSTEIN: OK, so let me try

978
00:57:13,930 --> 00:57:15,910
to repeat what I think the questions were.

979
00:57:15,910 --> 00:57:18,090
So the first one was, in this construction

980
00:57:18,090 --> 00:57:20,290
of this correspondence, why does the network

981
00:57:20,290 --> 00:57:22,050
need to be infinitely wide?

982
00:57:22,050 --> 00:57:25,730
And then I think the second question,

983
00:57:25,730 --> 00:57:29,098
if there is this correspondence, why do we actually do deep

984
00:57:29,098 --> 00:57:30,390
learning the way that we do it?

985
00:57:30,390 --> 00:57:32,598
Why don't we just extract what the kernel is and then

986
00:57:32,598 --> 00:57:35,370
just do Gaussian process regression

987
00:57:35,370 --> 00:57:37,650
or do some kernel method?

988
00:57:37,650 --> 00:57:39,550
So they're both really good questions.

989
00:57:39,550 --> 00:57:42,330
So on the first question, I'm going

990
00:57:42,330 --> 00:57:45,370
to talk a little bit about that on the next couple of slides.

991
00:57:45,370 --> 00:57:47,370
It's because the construction relies

992
00:57:47,370 --> 00:57:50,710
on a central limit theorem over the width of the network.

993
00:57:50,710 --> 00:57:51,790
That's the short answer.

994
00:57:51,790 --> 00:57:55,950
So for things to converge to this Gaussian distribution,

995
00:57:55,950 --> 00:57:57,630
it's because of a central limit theorem.

996
00:57:57,630 --> 00:57:59,350
And that requires something being large.

997
00:57:59,350 --> 00:58:02,730
And it turns out it's the width.

998
00:58:02,730 --> 00:58:06,100
The second question, yeah, that's a great question.

999
00:58:06,100 --> 00:58:07,850
Because some people really thought that we

1000
00:58:07,850 --> 00:58:09,770
should do kernel methods.

1001
00:58:09,770 --> 00:58:14,250
But it just seems to be not the case from the way

1002
00:58:14,250 --> 00:58:16,730
that we see things right.

1003
00:58:16,730 --> 00:58:18,770
It seems that even if you have an equivalent

1004
00:58:18,770 --> 00:58:22,430
kernel to your neural net, maybe for several different reasons,

1005
00:58:22,430 --> 00:58:24,930
you would actually rather just train the neural net and just

1006
00:58:24,930 --> 00:58:32,010
tune the hyperparameters rather than do the kernel method

1007
00:58:32,010 --> 00:58:33,750
or do the Gaussian process regression.

1008
00:58:33,750 --> 00:58:37,170
I think there's probably a few different potential reasons why

1009
00:58:37,170 --> 00:58:39,810
that's the case.

1010
00:58:39,810 --> 00:58:44,875
One is, well, first of all, the kernel method,

1011
00:58:44,875 --> 00:58:47,000
the Gaussian process ends up having hyperparameters

1012
00:58:47,000 --> 00:58:49,280
as well, because somehow the architecture of the network

1013
00:58:49,280 --> 00:58:50,863
is going to affect what the kernel is.

1014
00:58:50,863 --> 00:58:53,820
So it's not like it's really a tuning-free thing.

1015
00:58:56,360 --> 00:58:59,400
And then another thing is the kernel method scales

1016
00:58:59,400 --> 00:59:02,260
much worse with the size of your dataset than neural nets do.

1017
00:59:02,260 --> 00:59:04,160
I think it's the cost of all the methods

1018
00:59:04,160 --> 00:59:07,100
is cubic in the size of the training set,

1019
00:59:07,100 --> 00:59:12,640
whereas somehow neural nets scale linearly

1020
00:59:12,640 --> 00:59:15,000
with the size of your training data.

1021
00:59:15,000 --> 00:59:17,920
I'm not sure if, if you train an LLM,

1022
00:59:17,920 --> 00:59:20,440
you just train it once on the data.

1023
00:59:20,440 --> 00:59:22,400
And the computational cost is the cost

1024
00:59:22,400 --> 00:59:24,520
of a forward pass and a backward pass multiplied

1025
00:59:24,520 --> 00:59:26,020
by the size of your training set.

1026
00:59:26,020 --> 00:59:27,840
So it's definitely not a cubic dependence

1027
00:59:27,840 --> 00:59:29,340
on the number of training points.

1028
00:59:29,340 --> 00:59:31,180
So that's another thing.

1029
00:59:31,180 --> 00:59:32,060
But I don't know.

1030
00:59:32,060 --> 00:59:33,435
It's a super interesting question

1031
00:59:33,435 --> 00:59:35,740
why people use neural nets rather than kernel methods.

1032
00:59:35,740 --> 00:59:42,752
I think it may still be a somewhat open problem.

1033
00:59:42,752 --> 00:59:43,600
Yes, Zach?

1034
00:59:43,600 --> 00:59:45,180
AUDIENCE: I had two questions.

1035
00:59:45,180 --> 00:59:49,360
So the first is, here we're saying as the width approaches

1036
00:59:49,360 --> 00:59:51,880
infinity, the neural network is approaching the Gaussian

1037
00:59:51,880 --> 00:59:52,460
process.

1038
00:59:52,460 --> 00:59:53,377
JEREMY BERNSTEIN: Yes.

1039
00:59:53,377 --> 00:59:56,400
AUDIENCE: Have people been able to quantify

1040
00:59:56,400 --> 00:59:59,440
how close a network gets to a Gaussian process

1041
00:59:59,440 --> 01:00:01,465
if the width is finite?

1042
01:00:01,465 --> 01:00:02,840
JEREMY BERNSTEIN: So the question

