was, if this is a limiting result that only holds

1044
01:00:07,560 --> 01:00:10,340
at infinite width, if you have a finite-width network,

1045
01:00:10,340 --> 01:00:13,560
do we know what the deviations are from the limiting result?

1046
01:00:13,560 --> 01:00:17,140
I think there are papers on this question.

1047
01:00:22,260 --> 01:00:25,760
One answer is, even in the classic central limit theorem,

1048
01:00:25,760 --> 01:00:28,680
there's a result called the Berry-Esseen theorem, which

1049
01:00:28,680 --> 01:00:34,000
tells you about the deviations from normality

1050
01:00:34,000 --> 01:00:37,180
of a large sum of independent random variables and so on.

1051
01:00:37,180 --> 01:00:40,760
So you could just say, I'm going to take the Berry-Esseen theorem

1052
01:00:40,760 --> 01:00:43,580
and turn the handle and figure it out.

1053
01:00:43,580 --> 01:00:45,420
I don't quite know if that actually works.

1054
01:00:45,420 --> 01:00:47,188
AUDIENCE: What's the name of that theorem?

1055
01:00:47,188 --> 01:00:48,480
JEREMY BERNSTEIN: Like, Berry--

1056
01:00:48,480 --> 01:00:52,120
I'm probably not getting the name quite right, but Berry,

1057
01:00:52,120 --> 01:00:54,520
and then I think it's like Esseen.

1058
01:00:54,520 --> 01:00:55,435
AUDIENCE: OK.

1059
01:00:55,435 --> 01:00:57,560
JEREMY BERNSTEIN: I'm not sure how to pronounce it.

1060
01:00:57,560 --> 01:00:59,883
AUDIENCE: And then the other question

1061
01:00:59,883 --> 01:01:01,300
I had is-- earlier in the lecture,

1062
01:01:01,300 --> 01:01:02,967
you said that people were really, really

1063
01:01:02,967 --> 01:01:04,680
excited about this result-- and I'm

1064
01:01:04,680 --> 01:01:08,280
wondering what the larger effect on the research community

1065
01:01:08,280 --> 01:01:10,440
has been, how that's influencing how people

1066
01:01:10,440 --> 01:01:12,422
think about intelligent models?

1067
01:01:12,422 --> 01:01:14,380
JEREMY BERNSTEIN: Yes, that's a great question.

1068
01:01:14,380 --> 01:01:17,040
I think the short answer is that it's

1069
01:01:17,040 --> 01:01:20,880
had actually very little impact on what people actually

1070
01:01:20,880 --> 01:01:23,960
do in deep learning, realistically.

1071
01:01:23,960 --> 01:01:27,440
It may not be the case, but it seems that way.

1072
01:01:27,440 --> 01:01:30,000
Maybe at the end, I can talk a bit

1073
01:01:30,000 --> 01:01:33,840
about some of the potential limitations of the result.

1074
01:01:33,840 --> 01:01:38,840
But it's like, there could have been a version of deep learning

1075
01:01:38,840 --> 01:01:43,480
where you're like, ooh, OK, I need to solve this deep learning

1076
01:01:43,480 --> 01:01:44,400
problem.

1077
01:01:44,400 --> 01:01:47,063
Let me write down what my architecture is, take the limit,

1078
01:01:47,063 --> 01:01:48,480
work out what the kernel is, study

1079
01:01:48,480 --> 01:01:50,000
the properties of this kernel.

1080
01:01:50,000 --> 01:01:52,080
And, OK, now I'm ready to go.

1081
01:01:52,080 --> 01:01:53,400
But that's just not what it is.

1082
01:01:53,400 --> 01:01:56,080
It's more like, OK, just get an architecture that

1083
01:01:56,080 --> 01:01:59,080
seems reasonable and we'll just try it.

1084
01:01:59,080 --> 01:02:02,080
And I think there's a lesson, which

1085
01:02:02,080 --> 01:02:04,400
is that anything that's too convoluted of a procedure

1086
01:02:04,400 --> 01:02:07,000
is actually unlikely to be really capturing

1087
01:02:07,000 --> 01:02:07,860
what's going on.

1088
01:02:07,860 --> 01:02:11,520
And one thing is, you just don't need

1089
01:02:11,520 --> 01:02:13,880
to do that because you can just train the neural net

1090
01:02:13,880 --> 01:02:15,280
and you just see.

1091
01:02:15,280 --> 01:02:17,720
But I think there are also limitations

1092
01:02:17,720 --> 01:02:20,560
of this result that mean it's possibly also

1093
01:02:20,560 --> 01:02:22,100
potentially not that useful.

1094
01:02:26,262 --> 01:02:27,720
So the point of this lecture is not

1095
01:02:27,720 --> 01:02:31,200
to tell you this is something for your practical toolkit

1096
01:02:31,200 --> 01:02:32,620
for going out into the wild.

1097
01:02:32,620 --> 01:02:34,720
It's more like, if you're interested to really get

1098
01:02:34,720 --> 01:02:38,393
into the technical details of deep learning theory

1099
01:02:38,393 --> 01:02:40,310
and you want to think about the different ways

1100
01:02:40,310 --> 01:02:42,852
that people have come up with to think about neural networks,

1101
01:02:42,852 --> 01:02:44,170
here's one of them.

1102
01:02:47,750 --> 01:02:49,750
OK.

1103
01:02:49,750 --> 01:02:50,710
Yeah?

1104
01:02:50,710 --> 01:02:52,510
AUDIENCE: How many parameters do we

1105
01:02:52,510 --> 01:02:55,710
need to optimize in Gaussian process?

1106
01:02:55,710 --> 01:02:58,790
JEREMY BERNSTEIN: Well, generally,

1107
01:02:58,790 --> 01:03:01,750
the kernel, the covariance function

1108
01:03:01,750 --> 01:03:05,470
could have an arbitrary number of hyperparameters.

1109
01:03:05,470 --> 01:03:09,110
So if you're doing just straight-up Gaussian process

1110
01:03:09,110 --> 01:03:12,090
regression, you're going to pick a kernel function.

1111
01:03:12,090 --> 01:03:14,030
You get to design it and you get to choose how

1112
01:03:14,030 --> 01:03:15,950
many hyperparameters it has.

1113
01:03:15,950 --> 01:03:19,190
AUDIENCE: So what's the most critical kernel

1114
01:03:19,190 --> 01:03:20,190
function we choose?

1115
01:03:20,190 --> 01:03:23,990
And what is the correspondence to the neural network

1116
01:03:23,990 --> 01:03:25,825
architecture?

1117
01:03:25,825 --> 01:03:27,950
JEREMY BERNSTEIN: Yeah, I have an example coming up

1118
01:03:27,950 --> 01:03:33,070
for correspondence to the network architecture.

1119
01:03:33,070 --> 01:03:36,470
So the question was, is there an example of a typical kernel

1120
01:03:36,470 --> 01:03:40,390
that people use in Gaussian processes?

1121
01:03:40,390 --> 01:03:42,390
The first one that most people introduce

1122
01:03:42,390 --> 01:03:44,630
is just the squared exponential kernel,

1123
01:03:44,630 --> 01:03:49,870
which is e to the negative x minus x-prime squared.

1124
01:03:49,870 --> 01:03:51,950
And then you might introduce a hyperparameter,

1125
01:03:51,950 --> 01:03:54,190
like dividing by 2 sigma squared,

1126
01:03:54,190 --> 01:03:56,130
where sigma is a hyperparameter.

1127
01:03:56,130 --> 01:03:59,750
And it controls the length scale of the correlations

1128
01:03:59,750 --> 01:04:03,650
that the kernel-- and this is a kernel with one hyperparameter.

1129
01:04:03,650 --> 01:04:05,710
But then you could say, oh, what if x

1130
01:04:05,710 --> 01:04:07,630
is a multidimensional input.

1131
01:04:07,630 --> 01:04:09,830
Then something you could say is, what if I have

1132
01:04:09,830 --> 01:04:12,750
one sigma per input coordinate?

1133
01:04:12,750 --> 01:04:15,430
That would be a way to introduce more hyperparameters.

1134
01:04:15,430 --> 01:04:17,610
Or you could have a different functional form.

1135
01:04:17,610 --> 01:04:19,490
You could add something to this kernel.

1136
01:04:22,510 --> 01:04:24,990
That's the modeling decisions that people incur.

1137
01:04:24,990 --> 01:04:26,350
And you can read up papers.

1138
01:04:26,350 --> 01:04:29,470
And there'll be a table of 10 different kernels.

1139
01:04:29,470 --> 01:04:32,370
And they'll say, if your problem has this structure,

1140
01:04:32,370 --> 01:04:36,830
try kernel x; if it has this other structure, try kernel y.

1141
01:04:36,830 --> 01:04:40,670
It's similar to, oh, use a ConvNet for images

1142
01:04:40,670 --> 01:04:42,530
and use a transformer for this.

1143
01:04:42,530 --> 01:04:45,170
And it's just modeling decisions.

1144
01:04:45,170 --> 01:04:47,890
AUDIENCE: OK, thanks.

1145
01:04:47,890 --> 01:04:50,390
JEREMY BERNSTEIN: OK, I'm going to keep going, because now I

1146
01:04:50,390 --> 01:04:52,330
think we may run out of time.

1147
01:04:52,330 --> 01:04:53,510
I'm not sure.

1148
01:04:53,510 --> 01:04:54,970
So I didn't actually prove it.

1149
01:04:54,970 --> 01:04:56,490
I just wrote down a sketch.

1150
01:04:56,490 --> 01:04:58,510
And I think I'm just going to post

1151
01:04:58,510 --> 01:05:02,710
a reading about how to actually prove this correspondence.

1152
01:05:02,710 --> 01:05:07,550
But I'm just going to give you something high level about how

1153
01:05:07,550 --> 01:05:10,450
to prove that this correspondence happens.

1154
01:05:10,450 --> 01:05:11,950
So remember, the correspondence is

1155
01:05:11,950 --> 01:05:14,710
an infinitely wide neural network with random weights

1156
01:05:14,710 --> 01:05:17,510
induces the Gaussian processes--

1157
01:05:17,510 --> 01:05:20,230
the random functions you get are equivalent to a Gaussian

1158
01:05:20,230 --> 01:05:23,470
process.

1159
01:05:23,470 --> 01:05:25,070
And the main tool is something called

1160
01:05:25,070 --> 01:05:28,430
the multivariate central limit theorem, where mainly you

1161
01:05:28,430 --> 01:05:29,830
just hear about the central limit

1162
01:05:29,830 --> 01:05:34,150
theorem for a Gaussian random variable with one coordinate.

1163
01:05:34,150 --> 01:05:35,910
But it turns out there's a generalization

1164
01:05:35,910 --> 01:05:38,670
of the central limit theorem to Gaussian vectors.

1165
01:05:38,670 --> 01:05:41,270
And then proving this result is just

1166
01:05:41,270 --> 01:05:44,670
applying that theorem a couple of times

1167
01:05:44,670 --> 01:05:48,830
and doing the right induction over depth of the network.

1168
01:05:48,830 --> 01:05:51,730
And honestly, it's been a while since I thought about this,

1169
01:05:51,730 --> 01:05:55,830
but I think there's two steps.

1170
01:05:55,830 --> 01:05:57,450
There's two main steps.

1171
01:05:57,450 --> 01:06:01,470
One is, you pick a single input to the network,

1172
01:06:01,470 --> 01:06:05,350
and you inspect the network at a particular layer.

1173
01:06:05,350 --> 01:06:07,510
And you show that the activations

1174
01:06:07,510 --> 01:06:12,510
for that individual input are iid random variables, which

1175
01:06:12,510 --> 01:06:14,910
is kind of intuitive that they should be.

1176
01:06:14,910 --> 01:06:16,890
If you're randomly initializing the network,

1177
01:06:16,890 --> 01:06:21,030
the different activations of a particular layer

1178
01:06:21,030 --> 01:06:24,970
on the same input should somehow be iid random variables.

1179
01:06:24,970 --> 01:06:27,710
But you have to show that.

1180
01:06:27,710 --> 01:06:31,960
And then once you've shown that, you then consider that a layer,

1181
01:06:31,960 --> 01:06:33,820
and you consider batching the activations

1182
01:06:33,820 --> 01:06:35,520
over the multiple inputs.

1183
01:06:35,520 --> 01:06:39,900
And you think about each activation coordinate,

1184
01:06:39,900 --> 01:06:42,240
the batch of examples as being a random vector.

1185
01:06:42,240 --> 01:06:44,362
And then you apply the multivariate, essentially.

1186
01:06:44,362 --> 01:06:45,820
Then you think about the next layer

1187
01:06:45,820 --> 01:06:49,380
as summing over those random vectors somehow.

1188
01:06:49,380 --> 01:06:52,080
And you do this multivariate central limit theorem.

1189
01:06:52,080 --> 01:06:53,960
And anyway, I'm not really explaining it.

1190
01:06:53,960 --> 01:06:58,580
So the short answer is, there's a central limit theorem.

1191
01:06:58,580 --> 01:07:01,660
And I'll post a reading if someone wants

1192
01:07:01,660 --> 01:07:03,260
to go through the details.

1193
01:07:03,260 --> 01:07:04,240
Yeah, go ahead.

1194
01:07:07,420 --> 01:07:10,540
AUDIENCE: The first question is, as the weight goes

1195
01:07:10,540 --> 01:07:12,660
to infinity, you might be rescaling

1196
01:07:12,660 --> 01:07:15,180
the weights in some way, right, when you're sampling?

1197
01:07:15,180 --> 01:07:16,840
JEREMY BERNSTEIN: Yes, exactly.

1198
01:07:16,840 --> 01:07:18,420
AUDIENCE: [INAUDIBLE].

1199
01:07:18,420 --> 01:07:23,660
And the second question is, could you talk a little bit more

1200
01:07:23,660 --> 01:07:28,660
about why they're going to be independent random variables

1201
01:07:28,660 --> 01:07:31,260
when you have the complex dependencies

1202
01:07:31,260 --> 01:07:33,165
in the previous layers?

1203
01:07:33,165 --> 01:07:34,040
JEREMY BERNSTEIN: OK.

1204
01:07:34,040 --> 01:07:36,760
So maybe it's not obvious that they are independent.

1205
01:07:36,760 --> 01:07:39,560
And I think I maybe should not have said that.

1206
01:07:39,560 --> 01:07:41,740
So I think that's something you've got to show.

1207
01:07:41,740 --> 01:07:44,020
And I'm not going to go into it, but it's basically

1208
01:07:44,020 --> 01:07:45,860
because of the network being so wide

1209
01:07:45,860 --> 01:07:49,220
at every layer that you have a central limit theorem happening

1210
01:07:49,220 --> 01:07:50,320
at each layer.

1211
01:07:50,320 --> 01:07:52,120
And that's going to cause the independence.

1212
01:07:52,120 --> 01:07:55,640
But I'm not giving you a mechanism,

1213
01:07:55,640 --> 01:07:58,560
I'm just giving you a hand-wavy justification.

1214
01:07:58,560 --> 01:08:01,100
I'm going to post the reading.

1215
01:08:01,100 --> 01:08:03,780
So the other question was, what precisely

1216
01:08:03,780 --> 01:08:05,200
is the weight initialization?

1217
01:08:05,200 --> 01:08:07,460
How does it scale with the width?

1218
01:08:07,460 --> 01:08:10,740
So I think I have a concrete example

1219
01:08:10,740 --> 01:08:13,340
of this result in action, so let's just

1220
01:08:13,340 --> 01:08:16,180
go through it briefly.

1221
01:08:16,180 --> 01:08:18,340
So I think this is the instantiation

1222
01:08:18,340 --> 01:08:22,520
of the result for an MLP with a ReLU non-linearity.

1223
01:08:25,140 --> 01:08:27,300
And it turns out that all of the calculations,

1224
01:08:27,300 --> 01:08:30,500
you can solve them exactly for this model.

1225
01:08:30,500 --> 01:08:34,979
And actually, one of the papers in the reading

1226
01:08:34,979 --> 01:08:38,640
actually solves this kernel for this architecture.

1227
01:08:38,640 --> 01:08:41,580
But it's quite an old paper and it may not be that easy to read.

1228
01:08:41,580 --> 01:08:43,819
So I wonder if I can post something better

1229
01:08:43,819 --> 01:08:45,500
on Piazza, perhaps.

1230
01:08:45,500 --> 01:08:50,340
But basically, we set the nonlinearity to square root

1231
01:08:50,340 --> 01:08:58,979
2 times ReLU, and we sample the weights with variance 1

1232
01:08:58,979 --> 01:09:00,040
over fan-in.

1233
01:09:00,040 --> 01:09:04,700
So that's what we would call somehow

1234
01:09:04,700 --> 01:09:09,439
the standard parameterization in PyTorch, is doing 1 over fan-in.

1235
01:09:09,439 --> 01:09:10,973
It may actually not be the best way.

1236
01:09:10,973 --> 01:09:13,140
I actually think it's not the best way to initialize

1237
01:09:13,140 --> 01:09:14,160
the weights in practice.

1238
01:09:14,160 --> 01:09:17,260
But for this statement to hold, that's

1239
01:09:17,260 --> 01:09:21,399
the initialization you've got to use, so 1 over fan-in variance.

1240
01:09:24,420 --> 01:09:28,220
And then what you can prove is that the expectation

1241
01:09:28,220 --> 01:09:30,220
of the network output is 0.

1242
01:09:30,220 --> 01:09:33,220
This is a network with no nonlinearity on the output.

1243
01:09:33,220 --> 01:09:35,979
So it makes sense that it means 0.

1244
01:09:39,700 --> 01:09:41,520
Because the last layer is a linear layer,

1245
01:09:41,520 --> 01:09:43,260
the mean should be 0.

1246
01:09:43,260 --> 01:09:47,340
And then the covariance structure,

1247
01:09:47,340 --> 01:09:53,020
so the expectation of f of x multiplied with f of x prime

1248
01:09:53,020 --> 01:09:58,780
takes something called the compositional arccosine kernel.

1249
01:09:58,780 --> 01:10:01,700
So we take the dot product between the inputs.

1250
01:10:01,700 --> 01:10:04,340
And then we iteratively apply this function h.

1251
01:10:07,180 --> 01:10:11,420
We apply h L minus 1 times, where L minus 1 is the depth.

1252
01:10:11,420 --> 01:10:13,647
So it's called the compositional arccosine kernel.

1253
01:10:13,647 --> 01:10:15,480
It's just a particular mathematical function

1254
01:10:15,480 --> 01:10:17,860
which happens to correspond to this architecture.

1255
01:10:17,860 --> 01:10:20,580
Somebody proved it.

1256
01:10:20,580 --> 01:10:25,690
So this is an example of the result.

1257
01:10:25,690 --> 01:10:27,450
AUDIENCE: Actually, just a clarification.

1258
01:10:27,450 --> 01:10:30,090
The whole Gaussian process thing, this

1259
01:10:30,090 --> 01:10:32,453
is for untrained neural networks, right?

1260
01:10:32,453 --> 01:10:33,370
JEREMY BERNSTEIN: Yes.

1261
01:10:33,370 --> 01:10:33,690
AUDIENCE: OK.

1262
01:10:33,690 --> 01:10:36,090
JEREMY BERNSTEIN: The thing that I told you about, yes.

1263
01:10:36,090 --> 01:10:39,630
There is another thing, the neural tangent kernel,

1264
01:10:39,630 --> 01:10:43,250
which is supposed to be a Gaussian process

1265
01:10:43,250 --> 01:10:45,030
characterization of trained networks.

1266
01:10:45,030 --> 01:10:48,030
But it also holds under some assumptions.

1267
01:10:48,030 --> 01:10:51,170
And people have moved away from thinking too much

1268
01:10:51,170 --> 01:10:52,930
about that at the moment.

1269
01:10:52,930 --> 01:10:55,592
But, yeah, there's an analogous result,

1270
01:10:55,592 --> 01:10:57,550
which is supposed to hold for trained networks.

1271
01:10:57,550 --> 01:10:58,910
But I didn't talk about that.

1272
01:11:08,370 --> 01:11:12,330
OK, I think there's one slide, and then I

1273
01:11:12,330 --> 01:11:16,350
can take some questions if anyone has more questions.

1274
01:11:16,350 --> 01:11:21,150
But basically, if you really buy into this picture-- you're like,

1275
01:11:21,150 --> 01:11:27,650
yes, I believe in the Gaussian process correspondence and I

1276
01:11:27,650 --> 01:11:29,770
want to see how far I can take this--

1277
01:11:29,770 --> 01:11:31,850
you might have questions along the lines

1278
01:11:31,850 --> 01:11:36,530
of, how does the covariance kernel

1279
01:11:36,530 --> 01:11:39,490
or the covariance structure depend upon my choice of network

1280
01:11:39,490 --> 01:11:40,850
architecture?

1281
01:11:40,850 --> 01:11:45,130
How does it depend on the way I initialize the weights?

1282
01:11:45,130 --> 01:11:48,090
And could you actually use these considerations

1283
01:11:48,090 --> 01:11:51,650
to give you a theoretical way to inform

1284
01:11:51,650 --> 01:11:57,583
how we pick our architecture and how we regularize

1285
01:11:57,583 --> 01:11:58,750
the weights during training?

1286
01:11:58,750 --> 01:12:01,970
Or can this connection actually inform the way

1287
01:12:01,970 --> 01:12:03,710
that we do deep learning?

1288
01:12:03,710 --> 01:12:06,790
So that was the hope for this type of result.

1289
01:12:06,790 --> 01:12:09,570
And it seems to have just not really happened.

1290
01:12:09,570 --> 01:12:12,050
Instead, we just do transformer.

1291
01:12:12,050 --> 01:12:14,530
Or there's a list of architectures,

1292
01:12:14,530 --> 01:12:16,890
and you just pick one from that list.

1293
01:12:16,890 --> 01:12:22,850
And so it was a hope of having a formal, analytical way to do

1294
01:12:22,850 --> 01:12:25,910
design, principle design in deep learning.

1295
01:12:25,910 --> 01:12:29,990
But it doesn't seem that we actually use it.

1296
01:12:33,050 --> 01:12:35,675
So I had one thing, I have a blank slide,

1297
01:12:35,675 --> 01:12:37,050
there was one thing that I wanted

1298
01:12:37,050 --> 01:12:41,010
to talk about at the end, which was basically

1299
01:12:41,010 --> 01:12:44,090
this question of the weight initialization

1300
01:12:44,090 --> 01:12:46,130
that we talked about.

1301
01:12:46,130 --> 01:12:54,010
So that result held if we have a layer with a certain fan-in

1302
01:12:54,010 --> 01:13:02,170
and a certain fan-out, and we use this initialization 1

1303
01:13:02,170 --> 01:13:06,170
over fan-in, which is actually the PyTorch standard.

1304
01:13:06,170 --> 01:13:10,290
People call this Xavier initialization.

1305
01:13:10,290 --> 01:13:14,230
But it's not obviously actually the best thing to do,

1306
01:13:14,230 --> 01:13:16,870
although it is the thing that's built into PyTorch.

1307
01:13:19,530 --> 01:13:23,110
And I'm just going to give you one quick piece of intuition.

1308
01:13:30,090 --> 01:13:32,370
Wait, first of all, why do people

1309
01:13:32,370 --> 01:13:34,027
believe in this initialization?

1310
01:13:34,027 --> 01:13:36,110
Why did people come up with it in the first place?

1311
01:13:36,110 --> 01:13:38,402
And it's basically because it's the initialization that

1312
01:13:38,402 --> 01:13:40,770
is good at initialization.

1313
01:13:40,770 --> 01:13:45,352
And that's the same reason that we use it in the Neural Network

1314
01:13:45,352 --> 01:13:47,810
Gaussian Process Correspondence, because the correspondence

1315
01:13:47,810 --> 01:13:51,210
is about how networks behave at random initialization.

1316
01:13:51,210 --> 01:13:54,090
And basically, this is the way to initialize.

1317
01:13:54,090 --> 01:13:57,770
If you want to preserve activation magnitudes

1318
01:13:57,770 --> 01:14:01,450
at initialization, you should initialize this way.

1319
01:14:01,450 --> 01:14:08,970
But it ignores a fact of linear algebra that if you have

1320
01:14:08,970 --> 01:14:18,010
a matrix where the fan-in is much larger than the fan-out--

1321
01:14:18,010 --> 01:14:20,250
so the input dimension, the fan-in,

1322
01:14:20,250 --> 01:14:22,880
is much larger than the fan-out--

1323
01:14:22,880 --> 01:14:24,920
then the special fact about this matrix

1324
01:14:24,920 --> 01:14:28,360
is it has a huge null space, meaning that a lot of the inputs

1325
01:14:28,360 --> 01:14:30,320
are going to get mapped to 0.

1326
01:14:30,320 --> 01:14:33,880
And if you set up things such that at initialization,

1327
01:14:33,880 --> 01:14:38,420
the activation magnitudes get preserved-- well,

1328
01:14:38,420 --> 01:14:41,337
at initialization the vectors are hitting the null space,

1329
01:14:41,337 --> 01:14:43,920
and you're kind of scaling up all the part of the vector which

1330
01:14:43,920 --> 01:14:46,360
is not hitting the null space.

1331
01:14:46,360 --> 01:14:48,565
But actually, once you get further into training,

1332
01:14:48,565 --> 01:14:50,440
actually the inputs to the layer will kind of

1333
01:14:50,440 --> 01:14:52,440
align with the non-null space.

1334
01:14:52,440 --> 01:14:54,440
And then this principle is kind of bad

1335
01:14:54,440 --> 01:14:56,820
because then things are much too large.

1336
01:14:56,820 --> 01:14:58,560
Anyway, this is related to something

1337
01:14:58,560 --> 01:15:01,840
that people call maximal update parameterization or MUP, which

1338
01:15:01,840 --> 01:15:05,300
is something that if you're trying to train giant networks,

1339
01:15:05,300 --> 01:15:07,600
people care about this a lot.

1340
01:15:07,600 --> 01:15:09,420
Anyway, that's the end.

1341
